mw-file-description"><img resource="./File:1960-_Heat_wave_indicators_-_US.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/1960-_Heat_wave_indicators_-_US.svg/250px-1960-_Heat_wave_indicators_-_US.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/1960-_Heat_wave_indicators_-_US.svg/500px-1960-_Heat_wave_indicators_-_US.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1200" data-file-height="675" data-file-type="drawing" height="140" width="248" class="mw-file-element"/></a></span></div><div class="thumbcaption">US heat waves have increased in frequency, average duration, and intensity.<sup about="#mwt183" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-EPA_202406_48-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"EPA_202406"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-EPA_202406-48" id="mwAWc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAWg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWk">[</span>48<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWo">]</span></span></a></sup> <br/><br/>Also, heat wave <i>seasons</i> have grown in length.<sup about="#mwt187" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-EPA_202406_48-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"EPA_202406"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-EPA_202406-48"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-EPA_202406-48" id="mwAWs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAWw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAW0">[</span>48<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAW4">]</span></span></a></sup></div></div><div class="tsingle" style="width:188px;max-width:188px"><div class="thumbimage" style="height:139px;overflow:hidden"><span typeof="mw:File"><a href="./File:1960-_Annual_average_number_of_days_spent_in_heat_waves_-_US.svg" class="mw-file-description"><img resource="./File:1960-_Annual_average_number_of_days_spent_in_heat_waves_-_US.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/1960-_Annual_average_number_of_days_spent_in_heat_waves_-_US.svg/250px-1960-_Annual_average_number_of_days_spent_in_heat_waves_-_US.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/1960-_Annual_average_number_of_days_spent_in_heat_waves_-_US.svg/500px-1960-_Annual_average_number_of_days_spent_in_heat_waves_-_US.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1000" data-file-height="750" data-file-type="drawing" height="140" width="186" class="mw-file-element"/></a></span></div><div class="thumbcaption">Over decades, the average number of days spent in heat waves in the U.S. annually has increased, based on increases in both the average annual number of heat waves and on their average durations.<sup about="#mwt190" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-EPA_202406_48-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"EPA_202406"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-EPA_202406-48" id="mwAW8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAXA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXE">[</span>48<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXI">]</span></span></a></sup></div></div></div></div></div><span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt181"><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Pages_using_multiple_image_with_auto_scaled_images"/></span></div><meta typeof="mw:Includes/NoInclude/End" id="mwAXM"/>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAXQ"><a href="./File:1960-_Heat_storms.svg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAXU"><img resource="./File:1960-_Heat_storms.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/1960-_Heat_storms.svg/250px-1960-_Heat_storms.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/1960-_Heat_storms.svg/500px-1960-_Heat_storms.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1200" data-file-height="675" data-file-type="drawing" height="141" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwAXY"/></a><figcaption id="mwAXc">Heat storms—generally defined as heat waves of long duration and covering wide areas—have increased in recent decades, under one definition growing from five per year in the 1960s to 102 per year in the 2020s through September 2026.<sup about="#mwt192" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Reuters_20261020_49-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Reuters_20261020"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Reuters_20261020-49" id="mwAXg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAXk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXo">[</span>49<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXs">]</span></span></a></sup> Spikes in heat storms have often followed within months after <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./El_Niño–Southern_Oscillation" title="El Niño–Southern Oscillation" id="mwAXw">El Niño</a> events.<sup about="#mwt195" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Reuters_20261020_49-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Reuters_20261020"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Reuters_20261020-49"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Reuters_20261020-49" id="mwAX0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAX4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAX8">[</span>49<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYA">]</span></span></a></sup></figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwAYE">Heatwaves over land have become more frequent and more intense in almost all world regions since the 1950s, due to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Climate_change" title="Climate change" id="mwAYI">climate change</a>. Heat waves are more likely to occur simultaneously with droughts. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Marine_heatwaves" title="Marine heatwaves" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAYM">Marine heatwaves</a> are twice as likely as they were in 1980.<sup about="#mwt200" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-IPCC6AR_ExtremeEvents2_50-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"IPCC6AR_ExtremeEvents2"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-IPCC6AR_ExtremeEvents2-50"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-IPCC6AR_ExtremeEvents2-50" id="mwAYQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAYU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYY">[</span>50<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYc">]</span></span></a></sup> Climate change will lead to more very hot days and fewer very cold days.<sup about="#mwt201" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-IPCC-2013_51-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"IPCC-2013"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-IPCC-2013-51"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-IPCC-2013-51" id="mwAYg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAYk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYo">[</span>51<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYs">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt196" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"7"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAYw"><span title="Page / location: 7"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>7<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> There are fewer <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Cold_wave" title="Cold wave" id="mwAY0">cold waves</a>.<sup about="#mwt202" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-IPCC-2021_33-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"IPCC-2021"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-IPCC-2021-33" id="mwAY4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAY8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZA">[</span>33<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZE">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt197" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"8"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAZI"><span title="Page / location: 8"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>8<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup></p> 

<p id="mwAZM">Experts can often attribute the intensity of individual heat waves to global warming. Some extreme events would have been nearly impossible without human influence on the climate system. A heatwave that would occur once every ten years before global warming started now occurs 2.8 times as often. Under further warming, heatwaves are set to become more frequent. An event that would occur every ten years would occur every other year if global warming reaches <span about="#mwt203" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"2"},"2":{"wt":"C-change"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAZQ">2</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt203"> </span><span about="#mwt203">°C (3.6</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt203"> </span><span about="#mwt203">°F)</span>.<sup about="#mwt206" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-52" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-52"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-52" id="mwAZU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAZY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZc">[</span>52<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAZk"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Hyperthermia" title="Hyperthermia" id="mwAZo">Heat stress</a> is related to temperature.<sup about="#mwt211" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-53" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-53"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-53" id="mwAZs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAZw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZ0">[</span>53<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZ4">]</span></span></a></sup> It also increases if humidity is higher. The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Wet-bulb_temperature" title="Wet-bulb temperature" id="mwAZ8">wet-bulb temperature</a> measures both temperature and humidity. Humans cannot adapt to a wet-bulb temperature above <span about="#mwt207" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"35"},"2":{"wt":"C"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAaA">35</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt207"> </span><span about="#mwt207">°C (95</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt207"> </span><span about="#mwt207">°F)</span>. This heat stress can kill people. If global warming is kept below <span about="#mwt208" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1.5"},"2":{"wt":"or"},"3":{"wt":"2"},"4":{"wt":"C-change"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAaE">1.5 or 2</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt208"> </span><span about="#mwt208">°C (2.7 or 3.6</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt208"> </span><span about="#mwt208">°F)</span>, it will probably be possible to avoid this deadly heat and humidity in most of the tropics. But there may still be negative health impacts.<sup about="#mwt214" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-54" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-54"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-54" id="mwAaI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAaM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAaQ">[</span>54<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAaU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt217" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-55" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-55"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-55" id="mwAaY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAac"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAag">[</span>55<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAak">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAao">There is some evidence climate change is leading to a weakening of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Polar_vortex" title="Polar vortex" id="mwAas">polar vortex</a>. This would make the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Jet_stream" title="Jet stream" id="mwAaw">jet stream</a> more wavy.<sup about="#mwt221" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-56" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-56"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-56" id="mwAa0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAa4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAa8">[</span>56<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbA">]</span></span></a></sup> This would lead to outbursts of very cold winter weather across parts of Eurasia<sup about="#mwt224" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-57" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-57"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-57" id="mwAbE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAbI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbM">[</span>57<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbQ">]</span></span></a></sup> and North America and incursions of very warm air into the Arctic.<sup about="#mwt227" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-58" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-58"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-58" id="mwAbU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAbY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbc">[</span>58<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt230" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-59" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-59"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-59" id="mwAbk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAbo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbs">[</span>59<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt233" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-60" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-60"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-60" id="mwAb0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAb4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAb8">[</span>60<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAcA">]</span></span></a></sup> Some studies found a weakening of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Atlantic_meridional_overturning_circulation" title="Atlantic meridional overturning circulation" id="mwAcE">AMOC</a> by about 15% since 1950, causing <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Cold_blob" title="Cold blob" id="mwAcI">cooling in the North Atlantic</a> and warming in the Gulf Stream region.<sup about="#mwt236" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-61" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-61"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-61" id="mwAcM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAcQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAcU">[</span>61<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAcY">]</span></span></a></sup> Climate change is expected to weaken AMOC in all emissions scenarios<sup about="#mwt239" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-62" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-62"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-62" id="mwAcc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAcg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAck">[</span>62<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAco">]</span></span></a></sup> and, in some high emissions scenarios, can bring it to collapse. This can result in cooling of some parts of Europe by up to 30 degrees and warming in the southern hemisphere.<sup about="#mwt242" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-63" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-63"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-63" id="mwAcs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAcw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAc0">[</span>63<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAc4">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="4" id="mwAc8"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Rain">Rain</h3>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt243" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Effects of climate change on the water cycle"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAdA"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt244" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt243" id="mwAdE">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Effects_of_climate_change_on_the_water_cycle" title="Effects of climate change on the water cycle">Effects of climate change on the water cycle</a></div><p id="mwAdI">Warming increases global average <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Precipitation" title="Precipitation" id="mwAdM">precipitation</a>. Precipitation is when water vapour condenses out of clouds, such as rain and snow.<sup about="#mwt251" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-WG_1_chapter_8_64-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"WG 1 chapter 8"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-WG_1_chapter_8-64" id="mwAdQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAdU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAdY">[</span>64<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAdc">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt245" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1057"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAdg"><span title="Page / location: 1057"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>1057<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> Higher temperatures increase evaporation and surface drying. As the air warms it can hold more water. For every degree Celsius it can hold 7% more <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Water_vapour" title="Water vapour" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAdk">water vapour</a>.<sup about="#mwt252" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-WG_1_chapter_8_64-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"WG 1 chapter 8"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-WG_1_chapter_8-64" id="mwAdo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAds"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAdw">[</span>64<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAd0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt246" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1057"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAd4"><span title="Page / location: 1057"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>1057<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> Scientists have observed changes in the amount, intensity, frequency, and type of precipitation.<sup about="#mwt247" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEIPCC_SREX2012[httpsdocsgooglecomfiled0B1gFp6Ioo3akYklZcWkwWHJud00editpli1_8]_65-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEIPCC SREX2012[httpsdocsgooglecomfiled0B1gFp6Ioo3akYklZcWkwWHJud00editpli1 8]"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEIPCC_SREX2012[httpsdocsgooglecomfiled0B1gFp6Ioo3akYklZcWkwWHJud00editpli1_8]-65"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"IPCC SREX"},"2":{"wt":"2012"},"page":{"wt":"[https://docs.google.com/file/d/0B1gFp6Ioo3akYklZcWkwWHJud00/edit?pli=1 8]"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-FOOTNOTEIPCC_SREX2012[httpsdocsgooglecomfiled0B1gFp6Ioo3akYklZcWkwWHJud00editpli1_8]-65" id="mwAd8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAeA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAeE">[</span>65<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAeI">]</span></span></a></sup> Overall, climate change is causing longer hot dry spells, broken by more intense rainfall.<sup about="#mwt255" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Trenberth-2022_66-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Trenberth-2022"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Trenberth-2022-66"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Trenberth-2022-66" id="mwAeM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAeQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAeU">[</span>66<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAeY">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt250" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"151, 154"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAec"><span title="Page / location: 151, 154"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>151,<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>154<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup></p> 

<p id="mwAeg">Climate change has increased contrasts in rainfall amounts between wet and dry seasons. Wet seasons are getting wetter and dry seasons are getting drier. In the northern <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Polar_regions_of_Earth" title="Polar regions of Earth" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAek">high latitudes</a>, warming has also caused an increase in the amount of snow and rain.<sup about="#mwt258" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-WG_1_chapter_8_64-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"WG 1 chapter 8"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-WG_1_chapter_8-64" id="mwAeo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAes"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAew">[</span>64<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAe0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt256" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1057"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAe4"><span title="Page / location: 1057"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>1057<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> In the Southern Hemisphere, the rain associated with the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Storm_track" title="Storm track" id="mwAe8">storm tracks</a> has shifted south. Changes in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Monsoon" title="Monsoon" id="mwAfA">monsoons</a> vary a lot. More monsoon systems are becoming wetter than drier. In Asia summer monsoons are getting wetter. The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./West_African_monsoon" title="West African monsoon" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAfE">West African monsoon</a> is getting wetter over the central <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Sahel" title="Sahel" id="mwAfI">Sahel</a>, and drier in the far western Sahel.<sup about="#mwt259" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-WG_1_chapter_8_64-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"WG 1 chapter 8"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-WG_1_chapter_8-64" id="mwAfM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAfQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAfU">[</span>64<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAfY">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt257" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1058"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAfc"><span title="Page / location: 1058"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>1058<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="5" id="mwAfg"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Extreme_storms">Extreme storms</h3>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt260" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"multiple image ","href":"./Template:Multiple_image"},"params":{"total_width":{"wt":"450"},"image1":{"wt":"Wea02630 - Flickr - NOAA Photo Library.jpg"},"caption1":{"wt":"[[New Orleans]] submerged after [[Hurricane Katrina]], September 2005"},"image2":{"wt":"2024 Climate change increasing Atlantic hurricane peak wind speeds.svg"},"caption2":{"wt":"Water temperature increases caused by climate change intensified peak wind speeds in all 2024 Atlantic hurricanes.&lt;ref name=EnvRschClimate_20241120>● {{cite journal |last1=Gilford |first1=Daniel M. |last2=Giguere |first2=Joseph |last3=Pershing |first3=Andrew J. |title=Human-caused ocean warming has intensified recent hurricanes |journal=Environmental Research: Climate |date=20 November 2024 |volume=3 |issue=4 |page=045019 |doi=10.1088/2752-5295/ad8d02|bibcode=2024ERCli...3d5019G |doi-access=free }}&lt;br />● Explained in {{cite web |url=https://assets.ctfassets.net/cxgxgstp8r5d/gbIn7ANc1uAzb5cbwQMXC/58c45b96cac793be4cbc215502d4b4bd/Climate_Central_2024_hurricane_attribution_report.pdf |title=Climate change increased wind speeds for every 2024 Atlantic hurricane: Analysis |publisher=Climate Central |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241120111425/https://assets.ctfassets.net/cxgxgstp8r5d/gbIn7ANc1uAzb5cbwQMXC/58c45b96cac793be4cbc215502d4b4bd/Climate_Central_2024_hurricane_attribution_report.pdf |archive-date=20 November 2024 |date=20 November 2024 |url-status=live}}&lt;/ref>"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAfk"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1349637415/mw-parser-output/.tmulti" about="#mwt261" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Multiple image/styles.css","wrapper":".tmulti"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div class="thumb tmulti floatright" about="#mwt260"><div class="thumbinner multiimageinner" style="width:442px;max-width:442px"><div class="trow"><div class="tsingle" style="width:188px;max-width:188px"><div class="thumbimage" style="height:139px;overflow:hidden"><span typeof="mw:File"><a href="./File:Wea02630_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg" class="mw-file-description"><img resource="./File:Wea02630_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Wea02630_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg/250px-Wea02630_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Wea02630_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg/500px-Wea02630_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="2272" data-file-height="1704" data-file-type="bitmap" height="140" width="186" class="mw-file-element"/></a></span></div><div class="thumbcaption"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./New_Orleans" title="New Orleans">New Orleans</a> submerged after <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Hurricane_Katrina" title="Hurricane Katrina">Hurricane Katrina</a>, September 2005</div></div><div class="tsingle" style="width:250px;max-width:250px"><div class="thumbimage" style="height:139px;overflow:hidden"><span typeof="mw:File"><a href="./File:2024_Climate_change_increasing_Atlantic_hurricane_peak_wind_speeds.svg" class="mw-file-description"><img resource="./File:2024_Climate_change_increasing_Atlantic_hurricane_peak_wind_speeds.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/2024_Climate_change_increasing_Atlantic_hurricane_peak_wind_speeds.svg/250px-2024_Climate_change_increasing_Atlantic_hurricane_peak_wind_speeds.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/2024_Climate_change_increasing_Atlantic_hurricane_peak_wind_speeds.svg/500px-2024_Climate_change_increasing_Atlantic_hurricane_peak_wind_speeds.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1200" data-file-height="675" data-file-type="drawing" height="140" width="248" class="mw-file-element"/></a></span></div><div class="thumbcaption">Water temperature increases caused by climate change intensified peak wind speeds in all 2024 Atlantic hurricanes.<sup about="#mwt266" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-EnvRschClimate_20241120_67-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"EnvRschClimate_20241120"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-EnvRschClimate_20241120-67"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-EnvRschClimate_20241120-67" id="mwAfo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAfs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAfw">[</span>67<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAf0">]</span></span></a></sup></div></div></div></div></div><span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt260"><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Pages_using_multiple_image_with_auto_scaled_images"/></span>
<p id="mwAf4">Storms become wetter under climate change. These include <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Tropical_cyclones" title="Tropical cyclones" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAf8">tropical cyclones</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Extratropical_cyclones" title="Extratropical cyclones" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAgA">extratropical cyclones</a>. Both the maximum and mean rainfall rates increase. This more extreme rainfall is also true for <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Thunderstorm" title="Thunderstorm" id="mwAgE">thunderstorms</a> in some regions.<sup about="#mwt272" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-AR6WG1CH11_68-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"AR6WG1CH11"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-AR6WG1CH11-68"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-AR6WG1CH11-68" id="mwAgI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAgM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAgQ">[</span>68<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAgU">]</span></span></a></sup> Furthermore, tropical cyclones and storm tracks are moving towards the poles. This means some regions will see large changes in maximum wind speeds.<sup about="#mwt273" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-AR6WG1CH11_68-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"AR6WG1CH11"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-AR6WG1CH11-68" id="mwAgY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAgc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAgg">[</span>68<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAgk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt276" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Knutson-2019_69-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Knutson-2019"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Knutson-2019-69"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Knutson-2019-69" id="mwAgo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAgs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAgw">[</span>69<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAg0">]</span></span></a></sup> Scientists expect there will be fewer tropical cyclones, but they expect their strength to increase.<sup about="#mwt277" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Knutson-2019_69-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Knutson-2019"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Knutson-2019-69" id="mwAg4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAg8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAhA">[</span>69<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAhE">]</span></span></a></sup> There has probably been an increase in the number of tropical cyclones that intensify rapidly.<sup about="#mwt278" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-AR6WG1CH11_68-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"AR6WG1CH11"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-AR6WG1CH11-68" id="mwAhI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAhM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAhQ">[</span>68<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAhU">]</span></span></a></sup> Meteorological and seismological data indicate a widespread increase in wind-driven global ocean wave energy in recent decades that has been attributed to an increase in storm intensity over the oceans due to climate change.<sup about="#mwt281" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-70" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-70"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-70" id="mwAhY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAhc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAhg">[</span>70<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAhk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt284" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-71" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-71"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-71" id="mwAho"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAhs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAhw">[</span>71<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAh0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt287" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-72" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-72"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-72" id="mwAh4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAh8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAiA">[</span>72<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAiE">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Atmospheric_turbulence" title="Atmospheric turbulence" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAiI">Atmospheric turbulence</a> dangerous for aviation (hard to predict or that cannot be avoided by flying higher) probably increases due to climate change.<sup about="#mwt290" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-73" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-73"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-73" id="mwAiM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAiQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAiU">[</span>73<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAiY">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="6" id="mwAic"><h2 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Land">Land</h2>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt291" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"multiple image ","href":"./Template:Multiple_image"},"params":{"total_width":{"wt":"450"},"image1":{"wt":"Soil moisture and climate change.svg"},"caption1":{"wt":"The sixth IPCC Assessment Report included projections of changes in average soil moisture."},"image2":{"wt":"California Drought Dry Lakebed 2009.jpg"},"caption2":{"wt":"A dry lakebed in [[droughts in California|California]] In 2022, the state was experiencing its most serious drought in 1,200 years, worsened by climate change.&lt;ref>{{cite web |author=Irina Ivanova |date=2 June 2022 |title=California is rationing water amid its worst drought in 1,200 years |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/water-cutbacks-california-6-million-people-drought/ |access-date=2 June 2022 |publisher=[[CBS News]]}}&lt;/ref>"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAig"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1349637415/mw-parser-output/.tmulti" about="#mwt292" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Multiple image/styles.css","wrapper":".tmulti"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div class="thumb tmulti floatright" about="#mwt291"><div class="thumbinner multiimageinner" style="width:442px;max-width:442px"><div class="trow"><div class="tsingle" style="width:214px;max-width:214px"><div class="thumbimage" style="height:166px;overflow:hidden"><span typeof="mw:File"><a href="./File:Soil_moisture_and_climate_change.svg" class="mw-file-description"><img resource="./File:Soil_moisture_and_climate_change.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Soil_moisture_and_climate_change.svg/250px-Soil_moisture_and_climate_change.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Soil_moisture_and_climate_change.svg/500px-Soil_moisture_and_climate_change.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="960" data-file-height="755" data-file-type="drawing" height="167" width="212" class="mw-file-element"/></a></span></div><div class="thumbcaption">The sixth IPCC Assessment Report included projections of changes in average soil moisture.</div></div><div class="tsingle" style="width:224px;max-width:224px"><div class="thumbimage" style="height:166px;overflow:hidden"><span typeof="mw:File"><a href="./File:California_Drought_Dry_Lakebed_2009.jpg" class="mw-file-description"><img resource="./File:California_Drought_Dry_Lakebed_2009.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/California_Drought_Dry_Lakebed_2009.jpg/250px-California_Drought_Dry_Lakebed_2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/California_Drought_Dry_Lakebed_2009.jpg/500px-California_Drought_Dry_Lakebed_2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="700" data-file-height="525" data-file-type="bitmap" height="167" width="222" class="mw-file-element"/></a></span></div><div class="thumbcaption">A dry lakebed in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Droughts_in_California" title="Droughts in California">California</a> In 2022, the state was experiencing its most serious drought in 1,200 years, worsened by climate change.<sup about="#mwt295" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-74" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-74"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-74" id="mwAik"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAio"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAis">[</span>74<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAiw">]</span></span></a></sup></div></div></div></div></div><span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt291"><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Pages_using_multiple_image_with_auto_scaled_images"/></span>

<section data-mw-section-id="7" id="mwAi0"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Floods">Floods</h3>
<p id="mwAi4">Due to an increase in heavy rainfall events, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Floods" title="Floods" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAi8">floods</a> are likely to become more severe when they do occur.<sup about="#mwt301" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-WG_1_chapter_8_64-4" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"WG 1 chapter 8"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-WG_1_chapter_8-64" id="mwAjA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAjE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAjI">[</span>64<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAjM">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt298" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1155"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAjQ"><span title="Page / location: 1155"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>1155<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> The interactions between rainfall and flooding are complex. There are some regions in which flooding is expected to become rarer. This depends on several factors. These include changes in rain and snowmelt, but also <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Soil_moisture" title="Soil moisture" id="mwAjU">soil moisture</a>.<sup about="#mwt302" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-WG_1_chapter_8_64-5" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"WG 1 chapter 8"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-WG_1_chapter_8-64" id="mwAjY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAjc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAjg">[</span>64<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAjk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt299" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1156"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAjo"><span title="Page / location: 1156"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>1156<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> Climate change leaves soils drier in some areas, so they may absorb rainfall more quickly. This leads to less flooding. Dry soils can also become harder. In this case heavy rainfall runs off into rivers and lakes. This increases risks of flooding.<sup about="#mwt303" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-WG_1_chapter_8_64-6" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"WG 1 chapter 8"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-WG_1_chapter_8-64" id="mwAjs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAjw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAj0">[</span>64<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAj4">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt300" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1155"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAj8"><span title="Page / location: 1155"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>1155<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup></p> 

</section><section data-mw-section-id="8" id="mwAkA"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Droughts">Droughts</h3>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAkE"><a href="./File:1964-_Lake_Powell_water_level.svg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAkI"><img resource="./File:1964-_Lake_Powell_water_level.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/1964-_Lake_Powell_water_level.svg/250px-1964-_Lake_Powell_water_level.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/1964-_Lake_Powell_water_level.svg/500px-1964-_Lake_Powell_water_level.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1000" data-file-height="675" data-file-type="drawing" height="169" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwAkM"/></a><figcaption id="mwAkQ">In <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Southwestern_North_American_megadrought" title="Southwestern North American megadrought" id="mwAkU">Southwestern North American megadrought</a> starting about the year 2000, the water level of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Lake_Powell" title="Lake Powell" id="mwAkY">Lake Powell</a> declined from near maximum capacity to within <span about="#mwt304" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"30"},"2":{"wt":"ft"},"3":{"wt":"m"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAkc">30 feet (9.1</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt304"> </span><span about="#mwt304">m)</span> of the minimum level needed for <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Glen_Canyon_Dam" title="Glen Canyon Dam" id="mwAkg">Glen Canyon Dam</a> to generate electricity.<sup about="#mwt307" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Powell_Pool_1964_75-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Powell_Pool_1964"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Powell_Pool_1964-75"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Powell_Pool_1964-75" id="mwAkk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAko"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAks">[</span>75<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAkw">]</span></span></a></sup></figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwAk0">Climate change affects many factors associated with <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Drought" title="Drought" id="mwAk4">droughts</a>. These include how much rain falls and how fast the rain <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Evaporation" title="Evaporation" id="mwAk8">evaporates</a> again. Warming over land increases the severity and frequency of droughts around much of the world.<sup about="#mwt312" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-76" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-76"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-76" id="mwAlA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAlE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAlI">[</span>76<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAlM">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt315" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-WG_1_chapter_8_64-7" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"WG 1 chapter 8"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-WG_1_chapter_8-64"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-WG_1_chapter_8-64" id="mwAlQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAlU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAlY">[</span>64<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAlc">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt308" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1057"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAlg"><span title="Page / location: 1057"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>1057<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> In some tropical and subtropical regions of the world, there will probably be less rain due to global warming. This will make them more prone to drought. Droughts are set to worsen in many regions of the world. These include Central America, the Amazon and south-western South America. They also include West and Southern Africa. The Mediterranean and south-western Australia are also some of these regions.<sup about="#mwt316" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-WG_1_chapter_8_64-8" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"WG 1 chapter 8"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-WG_1_chapter_8-64" id="mwAlk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAlo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAls">[</span>64<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAlw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt309" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1157"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAl0"><span title="Page / location: 1157"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>1157<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup></p>

<p id="mwAl4">Higher temperatures increase evaporation. This dries the soil and increases <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Plant_stress_measurement" title="Plant stress measurement" id="mwAl8">plant stress</a>. Agriculture suffers as a result. This means even regions where overall rainfall is expected to remain relatively stable will experience these impacts.<sup about="#mwt319" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-WG_1_chapter_8_64-9" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"WG 1 chapter 8"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-WG_1_chapter_8-64" id="mwAmA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAmE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAmI">[</span>64<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAmM">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt317" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1157"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAmQ"><span title="Page / location: 1157"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>1157<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> These regions include central and northern Europe. Without climate change mitigation, around one third of land areas are likely to experience moderate or more severe drought by 2100.<sup about="#mwt320" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-WG_1_chapter_8_64-10" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"WG 1 chapter 8"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-WG_1_chapter_8-64" id="mwAmU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAmY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAmc">[</span>64<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAmg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt318" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1157"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAmk"><span title="Page / location: 1157"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>1157<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> Due to global warming droughts are more frequent and intense than in the past.<sup about="#mwt323" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-77" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-77"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-77" id="mwAmo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAms"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAmw">[</span>77<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAm0">]</span></span></a></sup></p> 

<p id="mwAm4">Several social factors may worsen the impact of droughts. These are increased water demand, population growth and urban expansion in many areas.<sup about="#mwt326" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-78" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-78"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-78" id="mwAm8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAnA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAnE">[</span>78<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAnI">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Land_restoration" title="Land restoration" id="mwAnM">Land restoration</a> techniques, such as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Agroforestry" title="Agroforestry" id="mwAnQ">agroforestry</a>, can help reduce the impact of droughts.<sup about="#mwt327" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Tsegai-2022_79-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Tsegai-2022"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Tsegai-2022-79"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Tsegai-2022-79" id="mwAnU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAnY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAnc">[</span>79<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAng">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="9" id="mwAnk"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Wildfires">Wildfires</h3>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt328" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Wildfire#Climate change effects"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAno"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt329" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt328" id="mwAns">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Wildfire#Climate_change_effects" title="Wildfire">Wildfire §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Climate change effects</a></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt330" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"multiple image ","href":"./Template:Multiple_image"},"params":{"total_width":{"wt":"450"},"image1":{"wt":"2003- Radiative power of wildfires.svg"},"caption1":{"wt":"A study published in June 2024 concluded that the frequency and intensity of extreme fire events more than doubled from 2003 to 2023. Extreme fire events have an outsized effect on the Earth system, even if area burned decreases.&lt;ref>{{cite web |last1=Cunningham |first1=Calum |last2=Bowman |first2=David |last3=Willliamson |first3=Grant |title=Extreme wildfires are on the rise globally, powered by the climate crisis |date=25 June 2024 |url=https://phys.org/news/2024-06-extreme-wildfires-globally-powered-climate.html |website=Phys.org |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240625183931/https://phys.org/news/2024-06-extreme-wildfires-globally-powered-climate.html |archive-date=25 June 2024 |url-status=live}} ●{{nbsp}}{{cite journal |last1=Cunningham |first1=Calum X. |last2=Williamson |first2=Grant J. |last3=Bowman |first3=David M. J. S. |title=Increasing frequency and intensity of the most extreme wildfires on Earth |journal=Nature Ecology &amp; Evolution |date=24 June 2024 |volume=8 |issue=8 |pages=1420–1425 |doi=10.1038/s41559-024-02452-2 |bibcode=2024NatEE...8.1420C }}&lt;/ref>"},"image2":{"wt":"1911- Wildfire disasters - worldwide.svg"},"caption2":{"wt":"Wildfire disasters (those claiming at least 10 lives or affecting over 100 people) have increased substantially in recent decades.&lt;ref name=AlJazeera_CRED_20210819/> Climate change intensifies heatwaves and droughts that dry vegetation, which in turn fuels wildfires.&lt;ref name=AlJazeera_CRED_20210819>{{cite news |last1=Haddad |first1=Mohammed |last2=Hussein |first2=Mohammed |title=Mapping wildfires around the world |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2021/8/19/mapping-wildfires-around-the-world-interactive |publisher=Al Jazeera |date=19 August 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210819102412/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2021/8/19/mapping-wildfires-around-the-world-interactive |archive-date=19 August 2021 |url-status=live }} Data source: [[Centre for Research on the Epidemiology of Disasters]].&lt;/ref>"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAnw"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1349637415/mw-parser-output/.tmulti" about="#mwt331" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Multiple image/styles.css","wrapper":".tmulti"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div class="thumb tmulti floatright" about="#mwt330"><div class="thumbinner multiimageinner" style="width:442px;max-width:442px"><div class="trow"><div class="tsingle" style="width:250px;max-width:250px"><div class="thumbimage" style="height:139px;overflow:hidden"><span typeof="mw:File"><a href="./File:2003-_Radiative_power_of_wildfires.svg" class="mw-file-description"><img resource="./File:2003-_Radiative_power_of_wildfires.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/2003-_Radiative_power_of_wildfires.svg/250px-2003-_Radiative_power_of_wildfires.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/2003-_Radiative_power_of_wildfires.svg/500px-2003-_Radiative_power_of_wildfires.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1200" data-file-height="675" data-file-type="drawing" height="140" width="248" class="mw-file-element"/></a></span></div><div class="thumbcaption">A study published in June 2024 concluded that the frequency and intensity of extreme fire events more than doubled from 2003 to 2023. Extreme fire events have an outsized effect on the Earth system, even if area burned decreases.<sup about="#mwt337" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-80" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-80"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-80" id="mwAn0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAn4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAn8">[</span>80<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAoA">]</span></span></a></sup></div></div><div class="tsingle" style="width:188px;max-width:188px"><div class="thumbimage" style="height:139px;overflow:hidden"><span typeof="mw:File"><a href="./File:1911-_Wildfire_disasters_-_worldwide.svg" class="mw-file-description"><img resource="./File:1911-_Wildfire_disasters_-_worldwide.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/1911-_Wildfire_disasters_-_worldwide.svg/250px-1911-_Wildfire_disasters_-_worldwide.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/1911-_Wildfire_disasters_-_worldwide.svg/500px-1911-_Wildfire_disasters_-_worldwide.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1000" data-file-height="750" data-file-type="drawing" height="140" width="186" class="mw-file-element"/></a></span></div><div class="thumbcaption">Wildfire disasters (those claiming at least 10 lives or affecting over 100 people) have increased substantially in recent decades.<sup about="#mwt341" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-AlJazeera_CRED_20210819_81-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"AlJazeera_CRED_20210819"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-AlJazeera_CRED_20210819-81" id="mwAoE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAoI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAoM">[</span>81<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAoQ">]</span></span></a></sup> Climate change intensifies heatwaves and droughts that dry vegetation, which in turn fuels wildfires.<sup about="#mwt345" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-AlJazeera_CRED_20210819_81-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"AlJazeera_CRED_20210819"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-AlJazeera_CRED_20210819-81"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-AlJazeera_CRED_20210819-81" id="mwAoU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAoY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAoc">[</span>81<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAog">]</span></span></a></sup></div></div></div></div></div><span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt330"><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Pages_using_multiple_image_with_auto_scaled_images"/></span>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt348" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"multiple image ","href":"./Template:Multiple_image"},"params":{"total_width":{"wt":"450"},"image3":{"wt":"2001- Forests net absorption of greenhouse gases - global.svg"},"caption3":{"wt":"Globally, [[wildfire]]s and [[deforestation]] have reduced forests&apos; net absorption of [[greenhouse gas]]es, reducing their effectiveness at mitigating climate change.&lt;ref name=WRI_Forest_Updake_20250724>{{cite web |last1=Harris |first1=Nancy |last2=Rose |first2=Melissa |title=World&apos;s Forest Carbon Sink Shrank to its Lowest Point in at Least 2 Decades, Due to Fires and Persistent Deforestation |url=https://www.wri.org/insights/forest-carbon-sink-shrinking-fires-deforestation |publisher=World Resources Institute |date=24 July 2025 }} Chart: \"Net Forest Carbon Sink (Gt CO&lt;sub>2&lt;/sub>e/yr)\"&lt;/ref> Global warming increases forest fires that release more greenhouse gases, creating a [[Positive feedback|feedback loop]] that causes more warming.&lt;ref name=NYTimes_20250724>{{cite news |last1=Mulkey |first1=Sachi Kitajima |last2=Stevens |first2=Harry |title=For 1st Time, Fires Are Biggest Threat to Forests&apos; Climate-Fighting Superpower |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/07/24/climate/wildfires-forest-carbon-sequestration.html |newspaper=The New York Times |date=24 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250725072642/https://www.nytimes.com/2025/07/24/climate/wildfires-forest-carbon-sequestration.html |archive-date=25 July 2025 |url-status=live }}&lt;/ref>"},"image4":{"wt":"2002- Area of forest damage due to fire.svg"},"caption4":{"wt":"Over recent decades, \"forest disturbance\" (damage) by fire has increased in most of the planet&apos;s forest zones.&lt;ref name=PNAS_20250721/> The increase in area, frequency, and severity of forest fires creates a [[positive feedback]] that increases global warming.&lt;ref name=PNAS_20250721>{{cite journal |last1=Potapov |first1=Peter |last2=Tyukavina |first2=Alexandra |last3=Turubanova |first3=Svetlana |last4=Hansen |first4=Matthew C. |last5=Giglio |first5=Louis |last6=Hernandez-Serna |first6=Andres |last7=Lima |first7=André |last8=Harris |first8=Nancy |last9=Stolle |first9=Fred |display-authors=4 |title=Unprecedentedly high global forest disturbance due to fire in 2023 and 2024 |journal=Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences |date=21 July 2025 |volume=122 |issue=30 |article-number=e2505418122 |doi=10.1073/pnas.2505418122 |pmid=40690667 |pmc=12318170 |bibcode=2025PNAS..12205418P }}&lt;/ref>"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAok"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1349637415/mw-parser-output/.tmulti" about="#mwt349" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Multiple image/styles.css","wrapper":".tmulti"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div class="thumb tmulti floatright" about="#mwt348"><div class="thumbinner multiimageinner" style="width:442px;max-width:442px"><div class="trow"><div class="tsingle" style="width:203px;max-width:203px"><div class="thumbimage" style="height:151px;overflow:hidden"><span typeof="mw:File"><a href="./File:2001-_Forests_net_absorption_of_greenhouse_gases_-_global.svg" class="mw-file-description"><img resource="./File:2001-_Forests_net_absorption_of_greenhouse_gases_-_global.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/2001-_Forests_net_absorption_of_greenhouse_gases_-_global.svg/250px-2001-_Forests_net_absorption_of_greenhouse_gases_-_global.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/2001-_Forests_net_absorption_of_greenhouse_gases_-_global.svg/500px-2001-_Forests_net_absorption_of_greenhouse_gases_-_global.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1000" data-file-height="750" data-file-type="drawing" height="151" width="201" class="mw-file-element"/></a></span></div><div class="thumbcaption">Globally, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Wildfire" title="Wildfire">wildfires</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Deforestation" title="Deforestation">deforestation</a> have reduced forests' net absorption of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Greenhouse_gas" title="Greenhouse gas">greenhouse gases</a>, reducing their effectiveness at mitigating climate change.<sup about="#mwt352" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-WRI_Forest_Updake_20250724_82-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"WRI_Forest_Updake_20250724"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-WRI_Forest_Updake_20250724-82"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-WRI_Forest_Updake_20250724-82" id="mwAoo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAos"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAow">[</span>82<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAo0">]</span></span></a></sup> Global warming increases forest fires that release more greenhouse gases, creating a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Positive_feedback" title="Positive feedback">feedback loop</a> that causes more warming.<sup about="#mwt357" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-NYTimes_20250724_83-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"NYTimes_20250724"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-NYTimes_20250724-83"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-NYTimes_20250724-83" id="mwAo4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAo8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwApA">[</span>83<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwApE">]</span></span></a></sup></div></div><div class="tsingle" style="width:235px;max-width:235px"><div class="thumbimage" style="height:151px;overflow:hidden"><span typeof="mw:File"><a href="./File:2002-_Area_of_forest_damage_due_to_fire.svg" class="mw-file-description"><img resource="./File:2002-_Area_of_forest_damage_due_to_fire.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/2002-_Area_of_forest_damage_due_to_fire.svg/250px-2002-_Area_of_forest_damage_due_to_fire.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/2002-_Area_of_forest_damage_due_to_fire.svg/500px-2002-_Area_of_forest_damage_due_to_fire.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1040" data-file-height="675" data-file-type="drawing" height="151" width="233" class="mw-file-element"/></a></span></div><div class="thumbcaption">Over recent decades, "forest disturbance" (damage) by fire has increased in most of the planet's forest zones.<sup about="#mwt360" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-PNAS_20250721_84-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"PNAS_20250721"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-PNAS_20250721-84" id="mwApI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwApM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwApQ">[</span>84<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwApU">]</span></span></a></sup> The increase in area, frequency, and severity of forest fires creates a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Positive_feedback" title="Positive feedback">positive feedback</a> that increases global warming.<sup about="#mwt364" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-PNAS_20250721_84-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"PNAS_20250721"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-PNAS_20250721-84"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-PNAS_20250721-84" id="mwApY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwApc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwApg">[</span>84<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwApk">]</span></span></a></sup></div></div></div></div></div><span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt348"><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Pages_using_multiple_image_with_auto_scaled_images"/></span>
<p id="mwApo">Climate change promotes <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Forest_fire_weather_index" title="Forest fire weather index" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAps">fire weather</a>, which makes <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Wildfire" title="Wildfire" id="mwApw">wildfires</a> more likely. </p>

<p id="mwAp0">Climate change can also increase the main natural ignition source of wildfire, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Lightning" title="Lightning" id="mwAp4">lightning</a>, and thereby increase the number of fires.<sup about="#mwt369" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-85" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-85"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-85" id="mwAp8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAqA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqE">[</span>85<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAqM">In some areas, an increase of wildfires has been attributed directly to climate change. Evidence from Earth's past also shows more fire in warmer periods.<sup about="#mwt372" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Jones-2022_86-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Jones-2022"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Jones-2022-86"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Jones-2022-86" id="mwAqQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAqU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqY">[</span>86<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqc">]</span></span></a></sup> Climate change increases <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Evapotranspiration" title="Evapotranspiration" id="mwAqg">evapotranspiration</a>. This can cause vegetation and soils to dry out. When a fire starts in an area with very dry vegetation, it can spread rapidly. Higher temperatures can also lengthen the fire season. This is the time of year in which severe wildfires are most likely, particularly in regions where snow is disappearing.<sup about="#mwt375" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Dunne-2020_87-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Dunne-2020"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Dunne-2020-87"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Dunne-2020-87" id="mwAqk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAqo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqs">[</span>87<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAqw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAq0">Weather conditions are raising the risks of wildfires. But the total area burnt by wildfires has decreased. This is mostly because <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Savanna" title="Savanna" id="mwAq4">savanna</a> has been converted to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Croplands" title="Croplands" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAq8">cropland</a>, so there are fewer trees to burn. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Prescribed_burning" title="Prescribed burning" class="mw-redirect" id="mwArA">Prescribed burning</a> is an indigenous practice in the US and Australia. It can reduce wildfire burning.<sup about="#mwt376" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Dunne-2020_87-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Dunne-2020"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Dunne-2020-87" id="mwArE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwArI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwArM">[</span>87<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwArQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p> 

<p id="mwArU">The carbon released from wildfires adds to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Carbon_dioxide_in_Earth's_atmosphere" title="Carbon dioxide in Earth's atmosphere" class="mw-redirect" id="mwArY">carbon dioxide in Earth's atmosphere</a> and therefore contributes to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Greenhouse_effect" title="Greenhouse effect" id="mwArc">greenhouse effect</a>. Climate models do not yet fully reflect this <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Climate_change_feedbacks" title="Climate change feedbacks" id="mwArg">climate change feedback</a>.<sup about="#mwt378" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-IPCC-2021_33-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"IPCC-2021"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-IPCC-2021-33" id="mwArk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAro"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwArs">[</span>33<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwArw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt377" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"20"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAr0"><span title="Page / location: 20"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>20<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="10" id="mwAr4"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Seismic_and_volcanic_activity">Seismic and volcanic activity</h3>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt379" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Glacial earthquake#Global warming"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAr8"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt380" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt379" id="mwAsA">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Glacial_earthquake#Global_warming" title="Glacial earthquake">Glacial earthquake §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Global warming</a></div>
<p id="mwAsE">In regions sensitive to climate change the frequency and intensity of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Types_of_volcanic_eruptions" title="Types of volcanic eruptions" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAsI">eruptions</a> will change as global warming increases. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Glacier_retreat" title="Glacier retreat" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAsM">Glacier retreat</a> and stronger precipitation can increase the chances for an eruption. As of 2024, government agencies are already addressing these changes and scientists are working to map the volcanoes most sensitive to climate change. The concerns regions are where <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Glacier_retreat" title="Glacier retreat" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAsQ">glaciers are melting fast</a>, and there are volcanoes heavily affected by precipitation. 716 volcanoes worldwide, may be affected by more extreme precipitation. Melting ice and extreme rainfall also increase secondary hazards, particularly <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Lahar" title="Lahar" id="mwAsU">lahars</a> and disturb eruption forecasting by inducing ground displacements.<sup about="#mwt383" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-88" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-88"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-88" id="mwAsY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAsc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAsg">[</span>88<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAsk">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAso">Earthquakes can be triggered by changes in the amount of stress on a fault in the Earth's crust. Strong rain, snow, drought and more pumping of groundwater by humans during droughts, can do it by increasing or reducing the weight of water on some pieces of the Earth's crust. So, as climate change will cause more extreme weather, it can induce more earthquakes. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Glacier_retreat" title="Glacier retreat" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAss">Glacier retreat</a> reduce stress loads on Earth's crust underneath, creating glacial earthquakes. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Glacial_earthquake" title="Glacial earthquake" id="mwAsw">Glacial earthquakes</a> in Greenland for example, peak in frequency in the summer months and are increasing over time, possibly in response to global warming.<sup about="#mwt386" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-89" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-89"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-89" id="mwAs0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAs4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAs8">[</span>89<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAtA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt389" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-90" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-90"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-90" id="mwAtE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAtI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAtM">[</span>90<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAtQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p> 

<p id="mwAtU"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Sea_level_rise" title="Sea level rise" id="mwAtY">Sea level rise</a> can also create pressure on tectonic faults, increasing risk for earthquakes.<sup about="#mwt392" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-91" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-91"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-91" id="mwAtc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAtg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAtk">[</span>91<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAto">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt395" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-92" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-92"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-92" id="mwAts"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAtw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAt0">[</span>92<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAt4">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAt8">In Greenland, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./2023_Greenland_landslide" title="2023 Greenland landslide" id="mwAuA">melting glaciers triggered a landslide</a>, which caused a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Mega-tsunami" title="Mega-tsunami" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAuE">mega-tsunami</a> in September 2023. Earthquake sensors around the world detected the resulting vibration, but the scale and duration of the event was unprecedented, so at first scientists failed to understand it. Further investigation revealed that the cause was the collapse of a 1,200-metre-high mountain peak into the remote <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Dickson_Fjord" title="Dickson Fjord" id="mwAuI">Dickson Fjord</a> on September 16, 2023, after the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Glacier" title="Glacier" id="mwAuM">glacier</a> below the mountain melted to a sufficient degree. The collapse into the fjord, in turn, launched a wave 200 metres high, which caused repeated movement of water back and forth in the fjord, generating seismic waves that were detectable worldwide for nine days.<sup about="#mwt396" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-93" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-93"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-93" id="mwAuQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAuU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAuY">[</span>93<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAuc">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="11" id="mwAug"><h2 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Oceans">Oceans</h2>
<figure class="mw-default-size skin-invert-image" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAuk"><a href="./File:WhereIsTheHeatOfGlobalWarming2023.svg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAuo"><img resource="./File:WhereIsTheHeatOfGlobalWarming2023.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/WhereIsTheHeatOfGlobalWarming2023.svg/500px-WhereIsTheHeatOfGlobalWarming2023.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/WhereIsTheHeatOfGlobalWarming2023.svg/960px-WhereIsTheHeatOfGlobalWarming2023.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="669" data-file-height="422" data-file-type="drawing" height="214" width="340" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.35" id="mwAus"/></a><figcaption id="mwAuw">Oceans have taken up almost 90% of the excess heat accumulated on Earth due to global warming.<sup about="#mwt399" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-94" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-94"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-94" id="mwAu0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAu4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAu8">[</span>94<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAvA">]</span></span></a></sup></figcaption></figure>
<figure class="mw-default-size skin-invert-image" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAvE"><a href="./File:Co2_time_series_aloha_08-09-2023.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAvI"><img resource="./File:Co2_time_series_aloha_08-09-2023.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Co2_time_series_aloha_08-09-2023.jpg/500px-Co2_time_series_aloha_08-09-2023.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Co2_time_series_aloha_08-09-2023.jpg/960px-Co2_time_series_aloha_08-09-2023.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="3238" data-file-height="2526" data-file-type="bitmap" height="265" width="340" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.35" id="mwAvM"/></a><figcaption id="mwAvQ">Climate change causes a drop in the ocean's pH value (called <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Ocean_acidification" title="Ocean acidification" id="mwAvU">ocean acidification</a>): Time series of atmospheric <span about="#mwt442" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"CO2","href":"./Template:CO2"},"params":{},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAvY">CO</span><sub style="font-size: 80%;vertical-align: -0.35em" about="#mwt442" id="mwAvc">2</sub> at Mauna Loa (in parts per million volume, ppmv; red), surface ocean p<span about="#mwt443" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"CO2","href":"./Template:CO2"},"params":{},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAvg">CO</span><sub style="font-size: 80%;vertical-align: -0.35em" about="#mwt443" id="mwAvk">2</sub> (μatm; green) and surface ocean pH (blue) at Ocean Station ALOHA in the subtropical North Pacific Ocean.<sup about="#mwt446" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-95" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-95"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-95" id="mwAvo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAvs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAvw">[</span>95<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAv0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt449" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-96" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-96"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-96" id="mwAv4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAv8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAwA">[</span>96<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAwE">]</span></span></a></sup></figcaption></figure><div class="excerpt-block" about="#mwt400" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"excerpt","href":"./Template:Excerpt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Effects of climate change on oceans"},"paragraphs":{"wt":"1-3"},"file":{"wt":"no"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAwI"><style data-mw-deduplicate="TemplateStyles:r1353838603" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" about="#mwt401" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Excerpt/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'>.mw-parser-output .excerpt-hat .mw-editsection-like{font-style:normal}</style><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt402" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable dablink excerpt-hat selfref">This section is an excerpt from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Effects_of_climate_change_on_oceans" title="Effects of climate change on oceans">Effects of climate change on oceans</a>.<span class="mw-editsection-like plainlinks"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a rel="mw:ExtLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Effects_of_climate_change_on_oceans&amp;action=edit" class="external text" data-mw-original-href="//en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Effects_of_climate_change_on_oceans&amp;action=edit">edit</a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div><div class="excerpt">
<p>One of the most important <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Effects_of_climate_change_on_oceans" title="Effects of climate change on oceans">effects of climate change on oceans</a> is an increase in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Ocean_temperature" title="Ocean temperature">ocean temperatures</a>. More frequent <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Marine_heatwave" title="Marine heatwave">marine heatwaves</a> are linked to this. The rising temperature contributes to a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Sea_level_rise" title="Sea level rise">rise in sea levels</a> due to the expansion of water as it warms and the melting of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Ice_sheet" title="Ice sheet">ice sheets</a> on land. Other effects on <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Ocean" title="Ocean">oceans</a> include <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Arctic_sea_ice_decline" title="Arctic sea ice decline">sea ice decline</a>, reduced <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Ocean_acidification" title="Ocean acidification">pH values</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Ocean_deoxygenation" title="Ocean deoxygenation">oxygen levels</a>, as well as increased <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Ocean_stratification" title="Ocean stratification">ocean stratification</a>. All this can lead to changes in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Ocean_currents" title="Ocean currents" class="mw-redirect">ocean currents</a>, for example, a weakening of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Atlantic_meridional_overturning_circulation" title="Atlantic meridional overturning circulation">Atlantic meridional overturning circulation</a> (AMOC).<sup about="#mwt405" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Effects_of_climate_change_on_oceans_SROCC_SPM_20190925_97-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Effects of climate change on oceans SROCC_SPM_20190925"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Effects_of_climate_change_on_oceans_SROCC_SPM_20190925-97"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Effects_of_climate_change_on_oceans_SROCC_SPM_20190925-97" id="mwAwM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAwQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAwU">[</span>97<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAwY">]</span></span></a></sup> The main cause of these changes is <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Emissions_of_greenhouse_gases" title="Emissions of greenhouse gases" class="mw-redirect">emissions of greenhouse gases</a> from human activities, mainly burning of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Fossil_fuel" title="Fossil fuel">fossil fuels</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Deforestation" title="Deforestation">deforestation</a>. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Carbon_dioxide" title="Carbon dioxide">Carbon dioxide</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Methane" title="Methane">methane</a> are examples of greenhouse gases. The additional <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Greenhouse_effect" title="Greenhouse effect">greenhouse effect</a> leads to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Ocean_heat_content" title="Ocean heat content">ocean warming</a> because the ocean takes up most of the additional heat in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Climate_system" title="Climate system">climate system</a>.<sup about="#mwt410" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-98" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-98"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-98" id="mwAwc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAwg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAwk">[</span>98<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAwo">]</span></span></a></sup> The ocean also absorbs some of the extra <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Carbon_dioxide_in_the_atmosphere" title="Carbon dioxide in the atmosphere" class="mw-redirect">carbon dioxide that is in the atmosphere</a>. This causes the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Ocean_acidification" title="Ocean acidification">pH value of the seawater to drop</a>.<sup about="#mwt416" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Effects_of_climate_change_on_oceans_:12_99-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Effects of climate change on oceans :12"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Effects_of_climate_change_on_oceans_:12-99"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Effects_of_climate_change_on_oceans_:12-99" id="mwAws"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAww"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAw0">[</span>99<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAw4">]</span></span></a></sup> Scientists estimate that the ocean absorbs about 25% of all human-caused CO<sub style="font-size: 80%;vertical-align: -0.35em">2</sub> emissions.<sup about="#mwt419" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Effects_of_climate_change_on_oceans_:12_99-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Effects of climate change on oceans :12"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Effects_of_climate_change_on_oceans_:12-99" id="mwAw8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAxA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAxE">[</span>99<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAxI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p>The various layers of the oceans have different temperatures. For example, the water is colder towards the bottom of the ocean. This temperature stratification will increase as the ocean surface warms due to rising air temperatures.<sup about="#mwt423" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Effects_of_climate_change_on_oceans_:10_100-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Effects of climate change on oceans :10"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Effects_of_climate_change_on_oceans_:10-100"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Effects_of_climate_change_on_oceans_:10-100" id="mwAxM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAxQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAxU">[</span>100<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAxY">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap"><span title="Page / location: 471"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>471<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> Connected to this is a decline in mixing of the ocean layers, so that warm water stabilises near the surface. A reduction of cold, deep <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Water_circulation" title="Water circulation" class="mw-redirect">water circulation</a> follows. The reduced vertical mixing makes it harder for the ocean to absorb heat. So a larger share of future warming goes into the atmosphere and land. One result is an increase in the amount of energy available for <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Tropical_cyclone" title="Tropical cyclone">tropical cyclones</a> and other storms. Another result is a decrease in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Nutrient" title="Nutrient">nutrients</a> for fish in the upper ocean layers. These changes also reduce the ocean's capacity to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Carbon_sink" title="Carbon sink">store carbon</a>.<sup about="#mwt427" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-101" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-101"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-101" id="mwAxc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAxg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAxk">[</span>101<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAxo">]</span></span></a></sup> At the same time, contrasts in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Salinity" title="Salinity">salinity</a> are increasing. Salty areas are becoming saltier and fresher areas less salty.<sup about="#mwt432" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Effects_of_climate_change_on_oceans_ChengSalinity_102-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Effects of climate change on oceans ChengSalinity"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Effects_of_climate_change_on_oceans_ChengSalinity-102"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Effects_of_climate_change_on_oceans_ChengSalinity-102" id="mwAxs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAxw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAx0">[</span>102<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAx4">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p>Warmer water cannot contain the same amount of oxygen as cold water. As a result, oxygen from the oceans moves to the atmosphere. Increased <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Ocean_stratification" title="Ocean stratification">thermal stratification</a> may reduce the supply of oxygen from surface waters to deeper waters. This lowers the water's oxygen content even more.<sup about="#mwt437" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-103" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-103"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-103" id="mwAx8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAyA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAyE">[</span>103<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAyI">]</span></span></a></sup> The ocean has already lost oxygen throughout its <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Water_column" title="Water column">water column</a>. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Oxygen_minimum_zone" title="Oxygen minimum zone">Oxygen minimum zones</a> are increasing in size worldwide.<sup about="#mwt440" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Effects_of_climate_change_on_oceans_:10_100-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Effects of climate change on oceans :10"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Effects_of_climate_change_on_oceans_:10-100" id="mwAyM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAyQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAyU">[</span>100<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAyY">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap"><span title="Page / location: 471"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>471<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup></p>
<link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_with_excerpts"/>
</div></div>

<section data-mw-section-id="12" id="mwAyc"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Sea_level_rise">Sea level rise</h3>
<figure class="mw-default-size skin-invert-image" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAyg"><a href="./File:1880-_Global_average_sea_level_rise_(SLR)_-_annually.svg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAyk"><img resource="./File:1880-_Global_average_sea_level_rise_(SLR)_-_annually.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/1880-_Global_average_sea_level_rise_%28SLR%29_-_annually.svg/500px-1880-_Global_average_sea_level_rise_%28SLR%29_-_annually.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/1880-_Global_average_sea_level_rise_%28SLR%29_-_annually.svg/960px-1880-_Global_average_sea_level_rise_%28SLR%29_-_annually.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1200" data-file-height="675" data-file-type="drawing" height="191" width="340" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.35" id="mwAyo"/></a><figcaption id="mwAys">The global average sea level has risen about <span about="#mwt450" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"250"},"2":{"wt":"mm"},"3":{"wt":"in"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAyw">250 millimetres (9.8</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt450"> </span><span about="#mwt450">in)</span> since 1880,<sup about="#mwt453" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-EPA_SLR_202207_104-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"EPA_SLR_202207"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-EPA_SLR_202207-104"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-EPA_SLR_202207-104" id="mwAy0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAy4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAy8">[</span>104<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAzA">]</span></span></a></sup> increasing the elevation on top of which other types of flooding (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Tidal_flooding" title="Tidal flooding" id="mwAzE">high-tide flooding</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Storm_surge" title="Storm surge" id="mwAzI">storm surge</a>) occur.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="mw-default-size skin-invert-image" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAzM"><a href="./File:2050_Projected_sea_level_rise_-_United_States_coasts_-_NOAA.svg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAzQ"><img resource="./File:2050_Projected_sea_level_rise_-_United_States_coasts_-_NOAA.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/2050_Projected_sea_level_rise_-_United_States_coasts_-_NOAA.svg/500px-2050_Projected_sea_level_rise_-_United_States_coasts_-_NOAA.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/2050_Projected_sea_level_rise_-_United_States_coasts_-_NOAA.svg/960px-2050_Projected_sea_level_rise_-_United_States_coasts_-_NOAA.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="900" data-file-height="650" data-file-type="drawing" height="246" width="340" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.35" id="mwAzU"/></a><figcaption id="mwAzY">Long-term sea level rise occurs in addition to intermittent tidal flooding. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./NOAA" title="NOAA" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAzc">NOAA</a> predicts different levels of sea level rise for coastlines within a single country.<sup about="#mwt456" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-NOAA_SeaLevelRiseTechReport_20220200_105-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"NOAA_SeaLevelRiseTechReport_20220200"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-NOAA_SeaLevelRiseTechReport_20220200-105"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-NOAA_SeaLevelRiseTechReport_20220200-105" id="mwAzg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAzk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAzo">[</span>105<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAzs">]</span></span></a></sup></figcaption></figure>
<div class="excerpt-block" about="#mwt457" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"excerpt","href":"./Template:Excerpt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"sea level rise"},"paragraphs":{"wt":"1-2"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwAzw"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1353838603" about="#mwt458" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Excerpt/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt459" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable dablink excerpt-hat selfref">This section is an excerpt from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Sea_level_rise" title="Sea level rise">Sea level rise</a>.<span class="mw-editsection-like plainlinks"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a rel="mw:ExtLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Sea_level_rise&amp;action=edit" class="external text" data-mw-original-href="//en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Sea_level_rise&amp;action=edit">edit</a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div><div class="excerpt">
<p>The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Sea_level" title="Sea level">sea level</a> has generally been rising since the end of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Last_Glacial_Maximum" title="Last Glacial Maximum">Last Glacial Maximum</a>, which was around 20,000 years ago.<sup about="#mwt462" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-106" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-106"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-106" id="mwAz0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAz4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAz8">[</span>106<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA0A">]</span></span></a></sup> Between 1901 and 2018, the global average sea level rose by 15–25<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>cm (6–10<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>in), with an increase of 2.3<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>mm (0.091<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>in) per year since the 1970s.<sup about="#mwt467" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Sea_level_rise_Fox-Kemper-2021_107-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Sea level rise Fox-Kemper-2021"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Sea_level_rise_Fox-Kemper-2021-107"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Sea_level_rise_Fox-Kemper-2021-107" id="mwA0E"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA0I"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA0M">[</span>107<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA0Q">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap"><span title="Page / location: 1216"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>1216<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> This was faster than the sea level had ever risen over at least the past 3,000 years.<sup about="#mwt470" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Sea_level_rise_Fox-Kemper-2021_107-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Sea level rise Fox-Kemper-2021"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Sea_level_rise_Fox-Kemper-2021-107" id="mwA0U"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA0Y"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA0c">[</span>107<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA0g">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap"><span title="Page / location: 1216"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>1216<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> The rate accelerated to 4.62<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>mm (0.182<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>in)/yr for the decade 2013–2022.<sup about="#mwt474" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Sea_level_rise_World_Meteorological_Organization-2023_108-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Sea level rise World Meteorological Organization-2023"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Sea_level_rise_World_Meteorological_Organization-2023-108"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Sea_level_rise_World_Meteorological_Organization-2023-108" id="mwA0k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA0o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA0s">[</span>108<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA0w">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Climate_change" title="Climate change">Climate change</a> due to human activities is the main cause of this persistent acceleration.<sup about="#mwt477" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Sea_level_rise_IPCC_109-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Sea level rise IPCC"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Sea_level_rise_IPCC-109" id="mwA00"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA04"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA08">[</span>109<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA1A">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap"><span title="Page / location: 5, 8"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>5,<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>8<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> Between 1993 and 2018, melting <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Ice_sheets" title="Ice sheets" class="mw-redirect">ice sheets</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Glacier" title="Glacier">glaciers</a> accounted for 44% of sea level rise, with another 42% resulting from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Thermal_expansion" title="Thermal expansion">thermal expansion</a> of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Water" title="Water">water</a>.<sup about="#mwt481" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Sea_level_rise_WCRP_Global_Sea_Level_Budget_Group-2018_110-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Sea level rise WCRP Global Sea Level Budget Group-2018"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Sea_level_rise_WCRP_Global_Sea_Level_Budget_Group-2018-110"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Sea_level_rise_WCRP_Global_Sea_Level_Budget_Group-2018-110" id="mwA1E"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA1I"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA1M">[</span>110<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA1Q">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap"><span title="Page / location: 1576"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>1576<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup></p>

<p>Sea level rise lags behind changes in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Earth" title="Earth">Earth</a>'s temperature by decades, and sea level rise will therefore continue to accelerate between now and 2050 in response to warming that has already happened.<sup about="#mwt486" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Sea_level_rise_National_Academies_of_Sciences,_Engineering,_and_Medicine-2011_111-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Sea level rise National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine-2011"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Sea_level_rise_National_Academies_of_Sciences,_Engineering,_and_Medicine-2011-111"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Sea_level_rise_National_Academies_of_Sciences,_Engineering,_and_Medicine-2011-111" id="mwA1U"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA1Y"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA1c">[</span>111<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA1g">]</span></span></a></sup> What happens after that depends on future human <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Greenhouse_gas_emissions" title="Greenhouse gas emissions">greenhouse gas emissions</a>. According to modelling in the early 2020s, if there are very deep cuts in emissions, sea level rise would slow down between 2050 and 2100. In that scenario, it would rise by between 30<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>cm (1<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>ft) and <style data-mw-deduplicate="TemplateStyles:r1154941027" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" about="#mwt489" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Fraction/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'>.mw-parser-output .frac{white-space:nowrap}.mw-parser-output .frac .num,.mw-parser-output .frac .den{font-size:80%;line-height:0;vertical-align:super}.mw-parser-output .frac .den{vertical-align:sub}.mw-parser-output .sr-only{border:0;clip:rect(0,0,0,0);clip-path:polygon(0px 0px,0px 0px,0px 0px);height:1px;margin:-1px;overflow:hidden;padding:0;position:absolute;width:1px}</style>1.0<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>m (<span class="frac">3<span class="sr-only">+</span><span class="num">1</span><span typeof="mw:Entity">⁄</span><span class="den">3</span></span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>ft) between the early 2020s and 2100, or by approximately 60<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>cm (2<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>ft) to <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1154941027" about="#mwt490" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Fraction/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/>130<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>cm (<span class="frac">4<span class="sr-only">+</span><span class="num">1</span><span typeof="mw:Entity">⁄</span><span class="den">2</span></span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>ft) from the 19th century to 2100. With high emissions it would instead accelerate further, and could rise by 50<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>cm (1.6<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>ft) or even by  1.9<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>m (6.2<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>ft) by 2100.<sup about="#mwt493" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-112" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-112"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-112" id="mwA1k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA1o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA1s">[</span>112<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA1w">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt496" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Sea_level_rise_IPCC_109-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Sea level rise IPCC"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Sea_level_rise_IPCC-109" id="mwA10"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA14"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA18">[</span>109<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA2A">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt498" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Sea_level_rise_Fox-Kemper-2021_107-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Sea level rise Fox-Kemper-2021"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Sea_level_rise_Fox-Kemper-2021-107" id="mwA2E"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA2I"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA2M">[</span>107<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA2Q">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap"><span title="Page: 1302"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>1302<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> In the long run, sea level rise would amount to 2–3<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>m (7–10<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>ft) over the next 2000 years if warming stays to its current 1.5<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>°C (2.7<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>°F) over the pre-industrial past. It would be 19–22 metres (62–72<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>ft) if warming peaks at 5<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>°C (9.0<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>°F).<sup about="#mwt504" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Sea_level_rise_IPCC_109-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Sea level rise IPCC"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Sea_level_rise_IPCC-109"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Sea_level_rise_IPCC-109" id="mwA2U"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA2Y"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA2c">[</span>109<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA2g">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap"><span title="Page / location: 21"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>21<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> The expected increase in flood hazard potential<sup class="noprint Inline-Template " style="margin-left:0.1em; white-space:nowrap;"><span typeof="mw:Entity">[</span><i><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Wikipedia:Please_clarify" title="Wikipedia:Please clarify"><span title="The text near this tag may need clarification or removal of jargon. (November 2026)">clarification needed</span></a></i><span typeof="mw:Entity">]</span></sup> is often exceedingly large even for modest sea-level rise scenarios, ranging from 10 to 1000 for rises of 0.5 m or less.<sup about="#mwt509" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-113" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-113"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-113" id="mwA2k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA2o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA2s">[</span>113<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA2w">]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_with_excerpts"/>
</div></div>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="13" id="mwA20"><h2 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Ice_and_snow">Ice and snow</h2>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt512" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"See also","href":"./Template:See_also"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Special Report on the Ocean and Cryosphere in a Changing Climate"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwA24"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt513" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt512" id="mwA28">See also: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Special_Report_on_the_Ocean_and_Cryosphere_in_a_Changing_Climate" title="Special Report on the Ocean and Cryosphere in a Changing Climate">Special Report on the Ocean and Cryosphere in a Changing Climate</a></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size skin-invert-image" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwA3A"><a href="./File:20210125_The_Cryosphere_-_Floating_and_grounded_ice_-_imbalance_-_climate_change.png" class="mw-file-description" id="mwA3E"><img resource="./File:20210125_The_Cryosphere_-_Floating_and_grounded_ice_-_imbalance_-_climate_change.png" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/20210125_The_Cryosphere_-_Floating_and_grounded_ice_-_imbalance_-_climate_change.png/500px-20210125_The_Cryosphere_-_Floating_and_grounded_ice_-_imbalance_-_climate_change.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/20210125_The_Cryosphere_-_Floating_and_grounded_ice_-_imbalance_-_climate_change.png/960px-20210125_The_Cryosphere_-_Floating_and_grounded_ice_-_imbalance_-_climate_change.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1889" data-file-height="1288" data-file-type="bitmap" height="232" width="340" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.35" id="mwA3I"/></a><figcaption id="mwA3M"> Earth lost 28 trillion tonnes of ice between 1994 and 2017, with melting grounded ice (ice sheets and glaciers) raising the global sea level by 34.6 ±3.1 mm.<sup about="#mwt514" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Cryosphere_20210125_114-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Cryosphere_20210125"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Cryosphere_20210125-114" id="mwA3Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA3U"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA3Y">[</span>114<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA3c">]</span></span></a></sup> The rate of ice loss has risen by 57% since the 1990s−from 0.8 to 1.2 trillion tonnes per year.<sup about="#mwt517" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Cryosphere_20210125_114-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Cryosphere_20210125"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Cryosphere_20210125-114"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Cryosphere_20210125-114" id="mwA3g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA3k"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA3o">[</span>114<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA3s">]</span></span></a></sup></figcaption></figure>
<figure class="mw-default-size skin-invert-image" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwA3w"><a href="./File:2015-2100_Impacts_of_global_warming_on_glaciers_and_sea_level_rise.svg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwA30"><img resource="./File:2015-2100_Impacts_of_global_warming_on_glaciers_and_sea_level_rise.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/2015-2100_Impacts_of_global_warming_on_glaciers_and_sea_level_rise.svg/500px-2015-2100_Impacts_of_global_warming_on_glaciers_and_sea_level_rise.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/2015-2100_Impacts_of_global_warming_on_glaciers_and_sea_level_rise.svg/960px-2015-2100_Impacts_of_global_warming_on_glaciers_and_sea_level_rise.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1200" data-file-height="800" data-file-type="drawing" height="227" width="340" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.35" id="mwA34"/></a><figcaption id="mwA38"> Melting of glacial mass is approximately linearly related to temperature rise.<sup about="#mwt520" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Science_20230105_115-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Science_20230105"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Science_20230105-115"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Science_20230105-115" id="mwA4A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA4E"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA4I">[</span>115<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA4M">]</span></span></a></sup></figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwA4Q">The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Cryosphere" title="Cryosphere" id="mwA4U">cryosphere</a>, the area of the Earth covered by snow or ice, is extremely sensitive to changes in global climate.<sup about="#mwt523" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-116" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-116"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-116" id="mwA4Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA4c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA4g">[</span>116<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA4k">]</span></span></a></sup> There has been an extensive loss of snow on land since 1981. Some of the largest declines have been observed in the spring.<sup about="#mwt526" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-117" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-117"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-117" id="mwA4o"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA4s"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA4w">[</span>117<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA40">]</span></span></a></sup> During the 21st century, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Snow_cover" title="Snow cover" class="mw-redirect" id="mwA44">snow cover</a> is projected to continue its retreat in almost all regions.<sup about="#mwt529" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-118" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-118"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-118" id="mwA48"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA5A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA5E">[</span>118<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA5I">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt521" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"39–69"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwA5M"><span title="Page / location: 39–69"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>39–69<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="14" id="mwA5Q"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Glaciers_decline">Glaciers decline</h3>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt530" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Retreat of glaciers since 1850"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwA5U"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt531" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt530" id="mwA5Y">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Retreat_of_glaciers_since_1850" title="Retreat of glaciers since 1850">Retreat of glaciers since 1850</a></div>
<p id="mwA5c">Since the beginning of the twentieth century, there has been a widespread <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Retreat_of_glaciers_since_1850" title="Retreat of glaciers since 1850" id="mwA5g">retreat of glaciers</a>.<sup about="#mwt534" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Fox-2021_119-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Fox-2021"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Fox-2021-119" id="mwA5k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA5o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA5s">[</span>119<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA5w">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt532" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1215"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwA50"><span title="Page / location: 1215"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>1215<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> Those glaciers that are not associated with the polar <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Ice_sheet" title="Ice sheet" id="mwA54">ice sheets</a> lost around 8% of their mass between 1971 and 2019.<sup about="#mwt535" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Fox-2021_119-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Fox-2021"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Fox-2021-119" id="mwA58"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA6A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA6E">[</span>119<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA6I">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt533" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1275"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwA6M"><span title="Page / location: 1275"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>1275<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> In the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Andes" title="Andes" id="mwA6Q">Andes</a> in South America and in the Himalayas in Asia, the retreat of glaciers could impact water supply.<sup about="#mwt538" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-120" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-120"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-120" id="mwA6U"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA6Y"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA6c">[</span>120<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA6g">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt541" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-121" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-121"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-121" id="mwA6k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA6o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA6s">[</span>121<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA6w">]</span></span></a></sup> The melting of those glaciers could also cause landslides or <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Glacial_lake_outburst_flood" title="Glacial lake outburst flood" id="mwA60">glacial lake outburst floods</a>.<sup about="#mwt544" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-122" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-122"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-122" id="mwA64"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA68"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA7A">[</span>122<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA7E">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="15" id="mwA7I"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Ice_sheets_decline">Ice sheets decline</h3>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt545" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Antarctic ice sheet#Changes due to climate change"},"2":{"wt":"Greenland ice sheet#Recent melting"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwA7M"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt546" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt545" id="mwA7Q">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Antarctic_ice_sheet#Changes_due_to_climate_change" title="Antarctic ice sheet">Antarctic ice sheet §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Changes due to climate change</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Greenland_ice_sheet#Recent_melting" title="Greenland ice sheet">Greenland ice sheet §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Recent melting</a></div>
<p id="mwA7U">The melting of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Greenland_ice_sheet" title="Greenland ice sheet" id="mwA7Y">Greenland</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./West_Antarctic_ice_sheet" title="West Antarctic ice sheet" class="mw-redirect" id="mwA7c">West Antarctic ice sheets</a> will continue to contribute to sea level rise over long time-scales. The Greenland ice sheet loss is mainly driven by melt from the top. Antarctic ice loss is driven by warm ocean water melting the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Outlet_glacier" title="Outlet glacier" class="mw-redirect" id="mwA7g">outlet glaciers</a>.<sup about="#mwt548" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Fox-2021_119-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Fox-2021"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Fox-2021-119" id="mwA7k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA7o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA7s">[</span>119<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA7w">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt547" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1215"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwA70"><span title="Page / location: 1215"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>1215<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup></p>

<p id="mwA74">Future melt of the West Antarctic ice sheet is potentially abrupt under a high emission scenario, as a consequence of a partial collapse.<sup about="#mwt555" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Collins-2019_123-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Collins-2019"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Collins-2019-123"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Collins-2019-123" id="mwA78"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA8A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA8E">[</span>123<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA8I">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt549" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"595–596"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwA8M"><span title="Page / location: 595–596"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>595–596<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> Part of the ice sheet is grounded on <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Bedrock" title="Bedrock" id="mwA8Q">bedrock</a> below sea level. This makes it possibly vulnerable to the self-enhancing process of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Marine_ice_sheet_instability" title="Marine ice sheet instability" class="mw-redirect" id="mwA8U">marine ice sheet instability</a>. <i id="mwA8Y">Marine ice cliff instability</i> could also contribute to a partial collapse. But there is limited evidence for its importance.<sup about="#mwt556" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Fox-2021_119-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Fox-2021"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Fox-2021-119" id="mwA8c"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA8g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA8k">[</span>119<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA8o">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt550" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1269–1270"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwA8s"><span title="Page / location: 1269–1270"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>1269–1270<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> A partial collapse of the ice sheet would lead to rapid sea level rise and a local decrease in ocean salinity. It would be irreversible for decades and possibly even millennia.<sup about="#mwt557" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Collins-2019_123-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Collins-2019"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Collins-2019-123" id="mwA8w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA80"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA84">[</span>123<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA88">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt551" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"595–596"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwA9A"><span title="Page / location: 595–596"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>595–596<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> The complete loss of the West Antarctic ice sheet would cause over <span about="#mwt552" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"5"},"2":{"wt":"m"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwA9E">5 metres (16</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt552"> </span><span about="#mwt552">ft)</span> of sea level rise.<sup about="#mwt560" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-124" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-124"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-124" id="mwA9I"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA9M"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA9Q">[</span>124<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA9U">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwA9Y">In contrast to the West Antarctic ice sheet, melt of the Greenland ice sheet is projected to take place more gradually over millennia.<sup about="#mwt567" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Collins-2019_123-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Collins-2019"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Collins-2019-123" id="mwA9c"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA9g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA9k">[</span>123<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA9o">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt561" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"595–596"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwA9s"><span title="Page / location: 595–596"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>595–596<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> Sustained warming between <span about="#mwt562" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1"},"2":{"wt":"C-change"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwA9w">1</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt562"> </span><span about="#mwt562">°C (1.8</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt562"> </span><span about="#mwt562">°F)</span> (low confidence) and <span about="#mwt563" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"4"},"2":{"wt":"C-change"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwA90">4</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt563"> </span><span about="#mwt563">°C (7.2</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt563"> </span><span about="#mwt563">°F)</span> (medium confidence) would lead to a complete loss of the ice sheet. This would contribute <span about="#mwt564" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"7"},"2":{"wt":"m"},"abbr":{"wt":"on"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwA94">7</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt564"> </span><span about="#mwt564">m (23</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt564"> </span><span about="#mwt564">ft)</span> to sea levels globally.<sup about="#mwt568" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Oppenheimer-2019_25-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Oppenheimer-2019"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Oppenheimer-2019-25" id="mwA98"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA-A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA-E">[</span>25<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA-I">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt565" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"363"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwA-M"><span title="Page / location: 363"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>363<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> The ice loss could become irreversible due to a further self-enhancing feedback. This is called the elevation-surface mass balance feedback. When ice melts on top of the ice sheet, the elevation drops. Air temperature is higher at lower altitudes, so this promotes further melting.<sup about="#mwt569" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Oppenheimer-2019_25-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Oppenheimer-2019"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Oppenheimer-2019-25" id="mwA-Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA-U"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA-Y">[</span>25<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA-c">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt566" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"362"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwA-g"><span title="Page / location: 362"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>362<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="16" id="mwA-k"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Sea_ice_decline">Sea ice decline</h3>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt570" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Arctic sea ice decline"},"2":{"wt":"Antarctic sea ice#Recent trends and climate change"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwA-o"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt571" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt570" id="mwA-s">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Arctic_sea_ice_decline" title="Arctic sea ice decline">Arctic sea ice decline</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Antarctic_sea_ice#Recent_trends_and_climate_change" title="Antarctic sea ice">Antarctic sea ice §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Recent trends and climate change</a></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size skin-invert-image" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwA-w"><a href="./File:1978-_Antarctic_sea_ice_extent_-_Purich_and_Doddridge.png" class="mw-file-description" id="mwA-0"><img resource="./File:1978-_Antarctic_sea_ice_extent_-_Purich_and_Doddridge.png" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/1978-_Antarctic_sea_ice_extent_-_Purich_and_Doddridge.png/500px-1978-_Antarctic_sea_ice_extent_-_Purich_and_Doddridge.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/1978-_Antarctic_sea_ice_extent_-_Purich_and_Doddridge.png/960px-1978-_Antarctic_sea_ice_extent_-_Purich_and_Doddridge.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="2628" data-file-height="1554" data-file-type="bitmap" height="201" width="340" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.35" id="mwA-4"/></a><figcaption id="mwA-8">Reporting the reduction in Antarctic sea ice extent in mid 2023, researchers concluded that a "regime shift" may be taking place "in which previously important relationships no longer dominate sea ice variability".<sup about="#mwt574" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-CommsEarthEnv_20230913_125-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"CommsEarthEnv_20230913"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-CommsEarthEnv_20230913-125"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-CommsEarthEnv_20230913-125" id="mwA_A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA_E"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA_I">[</span>125<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA_M">]</span></span></a></sup></figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwA_Q"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Sea_ice" title="Sea ice" id="mwA_U">Sea ice</a> reflects 50% to 70% of the incoming solar radiation back into space. Only 6% of incoming solar energy is reflected by the ocean.<sup about="#mwt577" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-126" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-126"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-126" id="mwA_Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA_c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA_g">[</span>126<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA_k">]</span></span></a></sup> As the climate warms, the area covered by snow or sea ice decreases. After sea ice melts, more energy is absorbed by the ocean, so it warms up. This <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Ice-albedo_feedback" title="Ice-albedo feedback" class="mw-redirect" id="mwA_o">ice-albedo feedback</a> is a self-reinforcing feedback of climate change.<sup about="#mwt580" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-127" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-127"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-127" id="mwA_s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwA_w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA_0">[</span>127<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwA_4">]</span></span></a></sup> Large-scale measurements of sea ice have only been possible since satellites came into use.<sup about="#mwt583" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-NOAA_2013_128-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"NOAA_2013"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-NOAA_2013-128"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-NOAA_2013-128" id="mwA_8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBAA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBAE">[</span>128<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBAI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBAM">Sea ice in the Arctic has declined in recent decades in area and volume due to climate change. It has been melting more in summer than it refreezes in winter. The decline of sea ice in the Arctic has been accelerating during the early twenty-first century. It has a rate of decline of 4.7% per decade. It has declined over 50% since the first satellite records.<sup about="#mwt590" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-129" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-129"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-129" id="mwBAQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBAU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBAY">[</span>129<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBAc">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt593" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Senftleben-2020_130-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Senftleben-2020"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Senftleben-2020-130"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Senftleben-2020-130" id="mwBAg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBAk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBAo">[</span>130<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBAs">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt596" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Yadav-2020_131-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Yadav-2020"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Yadav-2020-131"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Yadav-2020-131" id="mwBAw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBA0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBA4">[</span>131<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBA8">]</span></span></a></sup> Ice-free summers are expected to be rare at <span about="#mwt584" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1.5"},"2":{"wt":"C-change"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBBA">1.5</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt584"> </span><span about="#mwt584">°C (2.7</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt584"> </span><span about="#mwt584">°F)</span> degrees of warming. They are set to occur at least once every decade with a warming level of <span about="#mwt585" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"2"},"2":{"wt":"C-change"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBBE">2</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt585"> </span><span about="#mwt585">°C (3.6</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt585"> </span><span about="#mwt585">°F)</span>.<sup about="#mwt599" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-132" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-132"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-132" id="mwBBI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBBM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBBQ">[</span>132<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBBU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt586" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"8"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBBY"><span title="Page / location: 8"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>8<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> The Arctic will likely become ice-free at the end of some summers before 2050.<sup about="#mwt602" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Fox-2021_119-4" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Fox-2021"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Fox-2021-119"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Fox-2021-119" id="mwBBc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBBg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBBk">[</span>119<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBBo">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt587" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"9"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBBs"><span title="Page / location: 9"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>9<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup></p>

<p id="mwBBw">Sea ice extent in Antarctica varies a lot year by year. This makes it difficult to determine a trend, and record highs and record lows have been observed between 2013 and 2023. The general trend since 1979, the start of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Satellite_era" title="Satellite era" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBB0">satellite measurements</a>, has been roughly flat. Between 2015 and 2023, there has been a decline in sea ice, but due to the high variability, this does not correspond to a significant trend.<sup about="#mwt605" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-NOAA2023_133-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"NOAA2023"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-NOAA2023-133"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-NOAA2023-133" id="mwBB4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBB8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBCA">[</span>133<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBCE">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="17" id="mwBCI"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Permafrost_thawing">Permafrost thawing</h3>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt606" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Permafrost#Impacts of climate change"},"2":{"wt":"Climate change in Russia#Permafrost"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBCM"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt607" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt606" id="mwBCQ">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Permafrost#Impacts_of_climate_change" title="Permafrost">Permafrost §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Impacts of climate change</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Climate_change_in_Russia#Permafrost" title="Climate change in Russia">Climate change in Russia §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Permafrost</a></div>

<p id="mwBCU">Globally, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Permafrost" title="Permafrost" id="mwBCY">permafrost</a> warmed by about <span class="nowrap" about="#mwt608" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"val","href":"./Template:Val"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"0.3"},"u":{"wt":"degC"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBCc"><span data-sort-value="7002273450000000000♠"></span>0.3<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>°C</span> between 2007 and 2016. The extent of permafrost has been falling for decades. More decline is expected in the future.<sup about="#mwt612" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Fox-2021_119-5" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Fox-2021"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Fox-2021-119" id="mwBCg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBCk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBCo">[</span>119<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBCs">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt609" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1280"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBCw"><span title="Page / location: 1280"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>1280<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> Permafrost thaw makes the ground weaker and unstable. The thaw can seriously damage human infrastructure in permafrost areas such as railways, settlements and pipelines.<sup about="#mwt615" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-134" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-134"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-134" id="mwBC0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBC4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBC8">[</span>134<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBDA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt610" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"236"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBDE"><span title="Page / location: 236"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>236<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> Thawing soil can also release methane and <span about="#mwt611" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"CO2","href":"./Template:CO2"},"params":{},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBDI">CO</span><sub style="font-size: 80%;vertical-align: -0.35em" about="#mwt611" id="mwBDM">2</sub> from decomposing microbes. This can generate a strong <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Climate_change_feedback" title="Climate change feedback" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBDQ">feedback loop</a> to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Global_warming" title="Global warming" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBDU">global warming</a>.<sup about="#mwt618" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Koven_1877–1900_135-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Koven 1877–1900"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Koven_1877–1900-135"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Koven_1877–1900-135" id="mwBDY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBDc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBDg">[</span>135<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBDk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt621" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-136" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-136"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-136" id="mwBDo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBDs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBDw">[</span>136<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBD0">]</span></span></a></sup> Some scientists believe that <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Permafrost_carbon_cycle" title="Permafrost carbon cycle" id="mwBD4">carbon storage in permafrost</a> globally is approximately 1600 gigatons. This is twice the atmospheric pool.<sup about="#mwt624" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-137" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-137"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-137" id="mwBD8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBEA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBEE">[</span>137<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBEI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="18" id="mwBEM"><h2 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Wildlife_and_nature">Wildlife and nature</h2>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt625" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Effects of climate change on biomes"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBEQ"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt626" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt625" id="mwBEU">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Effects_of_climate_change_on_biomes" title="Effects of climate change on biomes">Effects of climate change on biomes</a></div>

<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt627" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"See also","href":"./Template:See_also"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Extinction risk from climate change"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBEY"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt628" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt627" id="mwBEc">See also: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Extinction_risk_from_climate_change" title="Extinction risk from climate change">Extinction risk from climate change</a></div><figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBEg"><a href="./File:Bleached_coral_(24577819729).jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBEk"><img resource="./File:Bleached_coral_(24577819729).jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Bleached_coral_%2824577819729%29.jpg/500px-Bleached_coral_%2824577819729%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Bleached_coral_%2824577819729%29.jpg/960px-Bleached_coral_%2824577819729%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="2433" data-file-height="1842" data-file-type="bitmap" height="257" width="340" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.35" id="mwBEo"/></a><figcaption id="mwBEs">Part of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Great_Barrier_Reef" title="Great Barrier Reef" id="mwBEw">Great Barrier Reef</a> in Australia in 2016 after a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Coral_bleaching" title="Coral bleaching" id="mwBE0">coral bleaching</a> event (partly caused by rising ocean temperatures and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Marine_heatwave" title="Marine heatwave" id="mwBE4">marine heatwaves</a>).</figcaption></figure><p id="mwBE8">Recent warming has had a big effect on natural biological systems.<sup about="#mwt630" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Rosenzweig-2007_138-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Rosenzweig-2007"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Rosenzweig-2007-138"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Rosenzweig-2007-138" id="mwBFA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBFE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFI">[</span>138<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFM">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt629" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"81"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBFQ"><span title="Page / location: 81"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>81<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Species" title="Species" id="mwBFU">Species</a> worldwide are moving poleward to colder areas. On land, species may move to higher elevations. Marine species find colder water at greater depths.<sup about="#mwt633" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Pecl-2017_10-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Pecl-2017"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Pecl-2017-10"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Pecl-2017-10" id="mwBFY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBFc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFg">[</span>10<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFk">]</span></span></a></sup> Climate change had the third biggest impact on nature out of various factors in the five decades up to 2020. Only change in land use and sea use and direct exploitation of organisms had a bigger impact.<sup about="#mwt636" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-139" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-139"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-139" id="mwBFo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBFs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFw">[</span>139<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBF0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBF4">The impacts of climate change on nature are likely to become bigger in the next few decades.<sup about="#mwt639" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-140" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-140"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-140" id="mwBF8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBGA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBGE">[</span>140<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBGI">]</span></span></a></sup> The stresses caused by climate change, combine with other stresses on ecological systems such as land conversion, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Land_degradation" title="Land degradation" id="mwBGM">land degradation</a>, harvesting, and pollution. They threaten substantial damage to unique ecosystems. They can even result in their complete loss and the extinction of species.<sup about="#mwt642" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-141" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-141"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-141" id="mwBGQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBGU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBGY">[</span>141<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBGc">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt645" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-142" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-142"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-142" id="mwBGg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBGk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBGo">[</span>142<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBGs">]</span></span></a></sup> This can disrupt key <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Species_interaction" title="Species interaction" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBGw">interactions between species</a> within ecosystems. This is because species from one location do not leave the warming habitat at the same rate. The result is rapid changes in the way the ecosystem functions.<sup about="#mwt646" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Pecl-2017_10-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Pecl-2017"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Pecl-2017-10" id="mwBG0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBG4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBG8">[</span>10<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHA">]</span></span></a></sup> Impacts include changes in regional rainfall patterns, earlier leafing of trees and plants over many regions, movements of species to higher latitudes and altitudes,<sup about="#mwt649" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-143" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-143"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-143" id="mwBHE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBHI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHM">[</span>143<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHQ">]</span></span></a></sup> changes in bird migrations, and shifting of the oceans' plankton and fish from cold- to warm-adapted communities.<sup about="#mwt652" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-NASA_2008_144-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"NASA_2008"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-NASA_2008-144"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-NASA_2008-144" id="mwBHU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBHY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHc">[</span>144<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBHk">These changes of land and ocean ecosystems have direct effects on human well-being.<sup about="#mwt657" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Parmesan-2022b_145-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Parmesan-2022b"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Parmesan-2022b-145"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Parmesan-2022b-145" id="mwBHo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBHs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHw">[</span>145<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBH0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt658" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Cooley-2022_146-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Cooley-2022"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Cooley-2022-146"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Cooley-2022-146" id="mwBH4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBH8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBIA">[</span>146<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBIE">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt653" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"385"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBII"><span title="Page / location: 385"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>385<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> For instance, ocean ecosystems help with coastal protection and provide food.<sup about="#mwt659" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Cooley-2022_146-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Cooley-2022"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Cooley-2022-146" id="mwBIM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBIQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBIU">[</span>146<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBIY">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt654" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"385"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBIc"><span title="Page / location: 385"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>385<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> Freshwater and land ecosystems can provide water for human consumption. Furthermore, these ecosystems can store carbon. This helps to stabilize the climate system.<sup about="#mwt660" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Parmesan-2022b_145-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Parmesan-2022b"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Parmesan-2022b-145" id="mwBIg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBIk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBIo">[</span>145<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBIs">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="19" id="mwBIw"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Ecosystems_on_land">Ecosystems on land</h3>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt661" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Effects of climate change on plant biodiversity"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBI0"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt662" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt661" id="mwBI4">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Effects_of_climate_change_on_plant_biodiversity" title="Effects of climate change on plant biodiversity">Effects of climate change on plant biodiversity</a></div>
<p id="mwBI8">Climate change is a major driver of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Biodiversity_loss" title="Biodiversity loss" id="mwBJA">biodiversity loss</a> in different land types. These include cool <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Conifer" title="Conifer" id="mwBJE">conifer</a> forests, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Savanna" title="Savanna" id="mwBJI">savannas</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Mediterranean_climate" title="Mediterranean climate" id="mwBJM">mediterranean-climate</a> systems, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Tropical_forest" title="Tropical forest" id="mwBJQ">tropical forests</a>, and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Arctic_tundra" title="Arctic tundra" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBJU">Arctic tundra</a>.<sup about="#mwt669" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Fischlin-2007_147-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Fischlin-2007"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Fischlin-2007-147"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Fischlin-2007-147" id="mwBJY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBJc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBJg">[</span>147<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBJk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt663" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"239"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBJo"><span title="Page / location: 239"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>239<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> In other ecosystems, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Land_use" title="Land use" id="mwBJs">land-use</a> change may be a stronger driver of biodiversity loss, at least in the near term.<sup about="#mwt670" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Fischlin-2007_147-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Fischlin-2007"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Fischlin-2007-147" id="mwBJw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBJ0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBJ4">[</span>147<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBJ8">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt664" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"239"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBKA"><span title="Page / location: 239"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>239<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> Beyond 2050, climate change may be the major cause of biodiversity loss globally.<sup about="#mwt671" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Fischlin-2007_147-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Fischlin-2007"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Fischlin-2007-147" id="mwBKE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBKI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKM">[</span>147<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt665" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"239"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBKU"><span title="Page / location: 239"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>239<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> Climate change interacts with other pressures. These include habitat modification, pollution and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Invasive_species" title="Invasive species" id="mwBKY">invasive species</a>. Through this interaction, climate change increases the risk of extinction for many terrestrial and freshwater species.<sup about="#mwt674" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-148" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-148"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-148" id="mwBKc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBKg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKk">[</span>148<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKo">]</span></span></a></sup> At <span about="#mwt666" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1.2"},"2":{"wt":"C-change"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBKs">1.2</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt666"> </span><span about="#mwt666">°C (2.2</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt666"> </span><span about="#mwt666">°F)</span> of warming (around 2023<sup about="#mwt677" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-149" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-149"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-149" id="mwBKw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBK0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBK4">[</span>149<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBK8">]</span></span></a></sup>) some ecosystems are threatened by mass die-offs of trees and from heatwaves.<sup about="#mwt680" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-150" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-150"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-150" id="mwBLA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBLE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLI">[</span>150<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLM">]</span></span></a></sup> At <span about="#mwt667" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"2"},"2":{"wt":"C-change"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBLQ">2</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt667"> </span><span about="#mwt667">°C (3.6</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt667"> </span><span about="#mwt667">°F)</span> of warming, around 10% of species on land would become critically endangered. This differs by group. For instance <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Insect" title="Insect" id="mwBLU">insects</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Salamander" title="Salamander" id="mwBLY">salamanders</a> are more vulnerable.<sup about="#mwt681" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Parmesan-2022a_12-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Parmesan-2022a"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Parmesan-2022a-12" id="mwBLc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBLg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLk">[</span>12<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLo">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt668" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"259"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBLs"><span title="Page / location: 259"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>259<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup></p>
<figure class="mw-default-size skin-invert-image" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBLw"><a href="./File:20210331_Global_tree_cover_loss_-_World_Resources_Institute.svg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBL0"><img resource="./File:20210331_Global_tree_cover_loss_-_World_Resources_Institute.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/20210331_Global_tree_cover_loss_-_World_Resources_Institute.svg/500px-20210331_Global_tree_cover_loss_-_World_Resources_Institute.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/20210331_Global_tree_cover_loss_-_World_Resources_Institute.svg/960px-20210331_Global_tree_cover_loss_-_World_Resources_Institute.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1286" data-file-height="750" data-file-type="drawing" height="198" width="340" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.35" id="mwBL4"/></a><figcaption id="mwBL8">The rate of global tree cover loss has approximately doubled since 2001, to an annual loss approaching an area the size of Italy.<sup about="#mwt684" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-WRIforestExtent_thru2025_151-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"WRIforestExtent_thru2025"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-WRIforestExtent_thru2025-151"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-WRIforestExtent_thru2025-151" id="mwBMA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBME"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBMI">[</span>151<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBMM">]</span></span></a></sup></figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwBMQ">Rainfall on the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Amazon_rainforest" title="Amazon rainforest" id="mwBMU">Amazon rainforest</a> is recycled when it <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Evaporation" title="Evaporation" id="mwBMY">evaporates</a> back into the atmosphere instead of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Surface_runoff" title="Surface runoff" id="mwBMc">running off</a> away from the rainforest. This water is essential for sustaining the rainforest. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Deforestation_of_the_Amazon_rainforest" title="Deforestation of the Amazon rainforest" id="mwBMg">Due to deforestation</a> the rainforest is losing this ability. This effect is even worse because climate change brings more frequent droughts to the area. The higher frequency of droughts in the first two decades of the 21st century and other data signal that a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Tipping_points_in_the_climate_system" title="Tipping points in the climate system" id="mwBMk">tipping point</a> from rainforest to savanna might be close. A 2019 study concluded that this ecosystem could begin a 50-year-long collapse to a savanna around 2021. After that it would become increasingly and disproportionally more difficult to prevent or reverse this shift.<sup about="#mwt687" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-152" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-152"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-152" id="mwBMo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBMs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBMw">[</span>152<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBM0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt690" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-153" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-153"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-153" id="mwBM4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBM8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNA">[</span>153<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNE">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt693" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-10.1038/s41467-020-15029-x_154-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"10.1038/s41467-020-15029-x"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-10.1038/s41467-020-15029-x-154"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-10.1038/s41467-020-15029-x-154" id="mwBNI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBNM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNQ">[</span>154<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNU">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="20" id="mwBNY"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Marine_ecosystems">Marine ecosystems</h3>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt694" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Effects of climate change on oceans#Impacts on marine life"},"2":{"wt":"Ocean acidification"},"3":{"wt":"Ocean deoxygenation"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBNc"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt695" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt694" id="mwBNg">Main articles: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Effects_of_climate_change_on_oceans#Impacts_on_marine_life" title="Effects of climate change on oceans">Effects of climate change on oceans §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Impacts on marine life</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Ocean_acidification" title="Ocean acidification">Ocean acidification</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Ocean_deoxygenation" title="Ocean deoxygenation">Ocean deoxygenation</a></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size skin-invert-image" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBNk"><a href="./File:Climate_change_threats_to_coral_reefs.png" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBNo"><img resource="./File:Climate_change_threats_to_coral_reefs.png" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Climate_change_threats_to_coral_reefs.png/500px-Climate_change_threats_to_coral_reefs.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Climate_change_threats_to_coral_reefs.png/960px-Climate_change_threats_to_coral_reefs.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1056" data-file-height="808" data-file-type="bitmap" height="260" width="340" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.35" id="mwBNs"/></a><figcaption id="mwBNw">Climate change will affect <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Coral_reef" title="Coral reef" id="mwBN0">coral reef</a> ecosystems, through <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Sea_level_rise" title="Sea level rise" id="mwBN4">sea level rise</a>, changes to the frequency and intensity of tropical storms, and altered ocean circulation patterns. When combined, all of these impacts dramatically alter ecosystem function, as well as the goods and services coral reef ecosystems provide.<sup about="#mwt698" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-155" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-155"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-155" id="mwBN8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBOA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBOE">[</span>155<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBOI">]</span></span></a></sup></figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwBOM"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Marine_heatwave" title="Marine heatwave" id="mwBOQ">Marine heatwaves</a> are happening more often. They have widespread impacts on life in the oceans. These include mass dying events and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Coral_bleaching" title="Coral bleaching" id="mwBOU">coral bleaching</a>.<sup about="#mwt702" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-156" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-156"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-156" id="mwBOY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBOc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBOg">[</span>156<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBOk">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Harmful_algal_bloom" title="Harmful algal bloom" id="mwBOo">Harmful algae blooms</a> have increased. This is in response to warming waters, loss of oxygen and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Eutrophication" title="Eutrophication" id="mwBOs">eutrophication</a>.<sup about="#mwt705" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-157" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-157"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-157" id="mwBOw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBO0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBO4">[</span>157<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBO8">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt699" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"451"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBPA"><span title="Page / location: 451"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>451<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> Melting sea ice destroys habitat, including for <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Algae" title="Algae" id="mwBPE">algae</a> that grows on its underside.<sup about="#mwt708" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Riebesell_158-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Riebesell"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Riebesell-158"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Riebesell-158" id="mwBPI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBPM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBPQ">[</span>158<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBPU">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBPY">Ocean acidification can harm marine organisms in various ways. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Marine_biogenic_calcification" title="Marine biogenic calcification" id="mwBPc">Shell-forming organisms</a> like <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Oyster" title="Oyster" id="mwBPg">oysters</a> are particularly vulnerable. Some <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Phytoplankton" title="Phytoplankton" id="mwBPk">phytoplankton</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Seagrass" title="Seagrass" id="mwBPo">seagrass</a> species may benefit. However, some of these are toxic to fish phytoplankton species. Their spread poses risks to fisheries and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Aquaculture" title="Aquaculture" id="mwBPs">aquaculture</a>. Fighting <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Pollution" title="Pollution" id="mwBPw">pollution</a> can reduce the impact of acidification.<sup about="#mwt711" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-159" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-159"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-159" id="mwBP0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBP4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBP8">[</span>159<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBQA">]</span></span></a></sup></p> 

<p id="mwBQE"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Coral_reef" title="Coral reef" id="mwBQI">Warm-water coral reefs</a> are very sensitive to global warming and ocean acidification. Coral reefs provide a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Habitat" title="Habitat" id="mwBQM">habitat</a> for thousands of species. They provide <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Ecosystem_services" title="Ecosystem services" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBQQ">ecosystem services</a> such as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Coastal_protection" title="Coastal protection" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBQU">coastal protection</a> and food. But 70–90% of today's warm-water coral reefs will disappear even if warming is kept to <span about="#mwt712" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1.5"},"2":{"wt":"C-change"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBQY">1.5</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt712"> </span><span about="#mwt712">°C (2.7</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt712"> </span><span about="#mwt712">°F)</span>.<sup about="#mwt717" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Hoegh-2018_160-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Hoegh-2018"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Hoegh-2018-160"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Hoegh-2018-160" id="mwBQc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBQg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBQk">[</span>160<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBQo">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt713" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"179"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBQs"><span title="Page / location: 179"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>179<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> Coral reefs are framework organisms. They build physical structures that form habitats for other sea creatures. Other framework organisms are also at risk from climate change. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Mangroves" title="Mangroves" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBQw">Mangroves</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Seagrass" title="Seagrass" id="mwBQ0">seagrass</a> are considered to be at moderate risk from lower levels of global warming.<sup about="#mwt718" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Hoegh-2018_160-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Hoegh-2018"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Hoegh-2018-160" id="mwBQ4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBQ8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBRA">[</span>160<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBRE">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt714" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"225"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBRI"><span title="Page / location: 225"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>225<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="21" id="mwBRM"><h2 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Tipping_points_and_irreversible_impacts">Tipping points and irreversible impacts</h2>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt719" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Tipping points in the climate system"},"2":{"wt":"Abrupt climate change"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBRQ"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt720" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt719" id="mwBRU">Main articles: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Tipping_points_in_the_climate_system" title="Tipping points in the climate system">Tipping points in the climate system</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Abrupt_climate_change" title="Abrupt climate change">Abrupt climate change</a></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBRY"><a href="./File:Tipping_points_2022_list.jpeg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBRc"><img resource="./File:Tipping_points_2022_list.jpeg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Tipping_points_2022_list.jpeg/500px-Tipping_points_2022_list.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Tipping_points_2022_list.jpeg/960px-Tipping_points_2022_list.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="3646" data-file-height="2375" data-file-type="bitmap" height="221" width="340" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.35" id="mwBRg"/></a><figcaption id="mwBRk">There are a number of places around the globe which can pass a tipping point around a certain level of warming, and eventually transition to a different state.<sup about="#mwt723" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-161" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-161"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-161" id="mwBRo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBRs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBRw">[</span>161<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBR0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt726" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-162" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-162"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-162" id="mwBR4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBR8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBSA">[</span>162<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBSE">]</span></span></a></sup></figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwBSI">The climate system exhibits "threshold behavior" or <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Tipping_points_in_the_climate_system" title="Tipping points in the climate system" id="mwBSM">tipping points</a> when parts of the natural environment enter into a new state. Examples are the runaway loss of ice sheets or the dieback of forests.<sup about="#mwt730" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:1_163-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":1"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-:1-163" id="mwBSQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBSU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBSY">[</span>163<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBSc">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt733" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-164" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-164"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-164" id="mwBSg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBSk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBSo">[</span>164<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBSs">]</span></span></a></sup> Tipping behavior is found in all parts of the climate system. These include ecosystems, ice sheets, and the circulation of the ocean and atmosphere.<sup about="#mwt736" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-165" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-165"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-165" id="mwBSw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBS0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBS4">[</span>165<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBS8">]</span></span></a></sup> Tipping points are studied using data from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Paleoclimatology" title="Paleoclimatology" id="mwBTA">Earth's distant past</a> and by physical modeling.<sup about="#mwt737" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:1_163-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":1"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-:1-163"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-:1-163" id="mwBTE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBTI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBTM">[</span>163<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBTQ">]</span></span></a></sup> There is already moderate risk of global tipping points at <span about="#mwt727" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"1"},"2":{"wt":"C-change"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBTU">1</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt727"> </span><span about="#mwt727">°C (1.8</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt727"> </span><span about="#mwt727">°F)</span> above pre-industrial temperatures. That becomes a high risk at <span about="#mwt728" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"2.5"},"2":{"wt":"C-change"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBTY">2.5</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt728"> </span><span about="#mwt728">°C (4.5</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt728"> </span><span about="#mwt728">°F)</span>.<sup about="#mwt738" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Hoegh-2018_160-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Hoegh-2018"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Hoegh-2018-160" id="mwBTc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBTg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBTk">[</span>160<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBTo">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt729" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"254, 258"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBTs"><span title="Page / location: 254, 258"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>254,<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>258<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> It is possible that some tipping points are close or have already been crossed. Examples are the West Antarctic and Greenland ice sheets, the Amazon rainforest, and warm-water coral reefs.<sup about="#mwt741" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-166" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-166"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-166" id="mwBTw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBT0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBT4">[</span>166<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBT8">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBUA">Tipping points are perhaps the most dangerous aspect of future climate change, potentially leading to irreversible impacts on society.<sup about="#mwt745" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-167" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-167"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-167" id="mwBUE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBUI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBUM">[</span>167<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBUQ">]</span></span></a></sup> A <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Atlantic_meridional_overturning_circulation#Slowdown_or_possible_shutdown_of_the_thermohaline_circulation" title="Atlantic meridional overturning circulation" id="mwBUU">collapse of the Atlantic meridional overturning circulation</a> would likely halve rainfall in India and lead to severe drops in temperature in Northern Europe.<sup about="#mwt748" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-168" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-168"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-168" id="mwBUY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBUc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBUg">[</span>168<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBUk">]</span></span></a></sup> Many tipping points are interlinked such that triggering one may lead to a cascade of effects.<sup about="#mwt751" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-169" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-169"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-169" id="mwBUo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBUs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBUw">[</span>169<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBU0">]</span></span></a></sup> This remains a possibility even well below <span about="#mwt742" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"2"},"2":{"wt":"C-change"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBU4">2</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt742"> </span><span about="#mwt742">°C (3.6</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt742"> </span><span about="#mwt742">°F)</span> of warming.<sup about="#mwt754" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-170" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-170"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-170" id="mwBU8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBVA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBVE">[</span>170<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBVI">]</span></span></a></sup> A 2018 study states that 45% of environmental problems, including those caused by climate change, are interconnected. This increases the risk of a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Domino_effect" title="Domino effect" id="mwBVM">domino effect</a>.<sup about="#mwt757" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-171" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-171"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-171" id="mwBVQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBVU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBVY">[</span>171<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBVc">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt760" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-172" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-172"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-172" id="mwBVg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBVk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBVo">[</span>172<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBVs">]</span></span></a></sup></p> 

<p id="mwBVw">Further impacts may be irreversible, at least over the timescale of many human generations.<sup about="#mwt764" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Schneider-2007_173-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Schneider-2007"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Schneider-2007-173"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Schneider-2007-173" id="mwBV0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBV4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBV8">[</span>173<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBWA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt761" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"785"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBWE"><span title="Page / location: 785"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>785<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> This includes warming of the deep ocean and acidification. These are set to continue even when global temperatures stop rising.<sup about="#mwt767" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-174" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-174"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-174" id="mwBWI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBWM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBWQ">[</span>174<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBWU">]</span></span></a></sup> In biological systems, the extinction of species would be an irreversible impact.<sup about="#mwt768" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Schneider-2007_173-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Schneider-2007"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Schneider-2007-173" id="mwBWY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBWc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBWg">[</span>173<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBWk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt762" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"785"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBWo"><span title="Page / location: 785"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>785<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> In social systems, unique <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Culture" title="Culture" id="mwBWs">cultures</a> may be lost.<sup about="#mwt769" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Schneider-2007_173-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Schneider-2007"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-Schneider-2007-173" id="mwBWw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBW0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBW4">[</span>173<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBW8">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt763" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"785"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwBXA"><span title="Page / location: 785"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>785<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> Climate change could make it more likely that endangered languages disappear.<sup about="#mwt772" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-climate_and_language_175-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"climate and language"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-climate_and_language-175"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-climate_and_language-175" id="mwBXE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBXI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBXM">[</span>175<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBXQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="22" id="mwBXU"><h2 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Health,_food_security_and_water_security"><span id="Health.2C_food_security_and_water_security" typeof="mw:FallbackId"></span>Health, food security and water security</h2>
<p id="mwBXY">Humans have a climate niche. This is a certain range of temperatures in which they flourish. Outside that niche, conditions are less favourable. This leads to negative effects on health, food security and more. This niche is a mean annual temperature below 29<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBXc"> </span>°C. As of May 2023, 60 million people lived outside this niche. With every additional 0.1 degree of warming, 140 million people will be pushed out of it.<sup about="#mwt775" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-176" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-176"}}'><a href="./Effects_of_climate_change#cite_note-176" id="mwBXg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBXk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBXo">[</span>176<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBXs">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="23" id="mwBXw"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Health">Health</h3>
